t naming and template resolution mechanisms maintain independence from the underlying object lookup strategy?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 59171, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_004_code-review-graph.json"}
{"task_id": "django_004", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "serena", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "`DetailViewTest` uses isolated mixin contract tests with minimal, local test subclasses.\n\nIn [tests/generic_views/test_detail.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/tests/generic_views/test_detail.py:185), two tests define small inner classes and inject an already-resolved `Author` object loaded through `Author.objects.defer(\"name\")`:\n\n- `test_deferred_queryset_template_name()` subclasses `SingleObjectTemplateResponseMixin` and directly calls `get_template_names()`.\n- `test_deferred_queryset_context_object_name()` subclasses `ModelFormMixin` and directly calls `get_context_data()`.\n\nThis deliberately bypasses `DetailView` dispatch and `SingleObjectMixin.get_object()`. The deferred ORM lookup is merely how the object is obtained; afterward, the tested mechanisms derive metadata from the model instance:\n\n- `SingleObjectTemplateResponseMixin.get_template_names()` uses `self.object._meta` to produce `generic_views/author_detail.html` in [django/views/generic/detail.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/views/generic/detail.py:121).\n- `SingleObjectMixin.get_context_object_name()` uses `obj._meta.model_name`, while `get_context_data()` adds both `\"object\"` and `\"author\"` in [django/views/generic/detail.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/views/generic/detail.py:83).\n\nArchitecturally, this is separation of concerns through mixin composition, verified with focused test doubles and dependency injection. It demonstrates that presentation concerns\u2014context naming and template resolution\u2014depend on the resolved object\u2019s model metadata, not on whether the object came from a normal, deferred, PK, slug, or other lookup strategy.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 8, "completeness": 6, "relevance": 6, "clarity": 8, "reasoning": 7}, "input_tokens": 25092, "output_tokens": 1189, "total_tokens": 26281, "cache_read_tokens": 153344, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 13, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": [], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "serena", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "serena", "server_name": "serena", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/serena.exe", "launch_args": ["start-mcp-server", "--project", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-serena-django-django", "--transport", "stdio", "--enable-web-dashboard", "false", "--enable-gui-log-window", "false"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__get_symbols_overview", "mcp__serena__find_declaration", "mcp__serena__find_implementations", "mcp__serena__find_referencing_symbols", "mcp__serena__get_diagnostics_for_file", "mcp__serena__find_file", "mcp__serena__list_dir", "mcp__serena__read_file"], "index": null, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-serena-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["activate_project", "create_text_file", "delete_memory", "edit_memory", "execute_shell_command", "find_declaration", "find_file", "find_implementations", "find_referencing_symbols", "find_symbol", "get_current_config", "get_diagnostics_for_file", "get_symbols_overview", "initial_instructions", "insert_after_symbol", "insert_before_symbol", "list_dir", "list_memories", "onboarding", "read_file", "read_memory", "rename_memory", "rename_symbol", "replace_content", "replace_in_files", "replace_symbol_body", "safe_delete_symbol", "search_for_pattern", "write_memory"], "served_count": 29, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__serena__initial_instructions", "mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"serena": {"ok": 13, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"skipped": "no-index-by-design", "seconds": 0.0}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 56.829094648361206, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 5.597868204116821, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.06242299999999999, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T13:58:37.612365+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: What architectural pattern does DetailViewTest employ to verify that context object naming and template resolution mechanisms maintain independence from the underlying object lookup strategy?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 56827, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_004_serena.json"}
{"task_id": "django_006", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "c0-bare", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The `Person` model uses an adjacency-list graph pattern: each row contains two nullable foreign keys back to the same table.\n\nIn [tests/m2o_recursive/models.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/tests/m2o_recursive/models.py:26):\n\n- `mother = ForeignKey(\"self\", SET_NULL, null=True, related_name=\"mothers_child_set\")`\n- `father = ForeignKey(\"self\", SET_NULL, null=True, related_name=\"fathers_child_set\")`\n\nThis creates two directed, role-specific edges between `Person` nodes. Traversal works in both directions:\n\n- Ancestors through `person.mother` and `person.father`.\n- Descendants through `person.mothers_child_set` and `person.fathers_child_set`.\n\nThe distinct reverse names are architecturally necessary because two foreign keys target the same model. Their behavior is demonstrated in [tests/m2o_recursive/tests.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/tests/m2o_recursive/tests.py:34), which accesses both forward parent links and both reverse child managers.\n\nData integrity comes from normal `ForeignKey` behavior: it targets the related row\u2019s primary key, is indexed, and has `db_constraint=True` by default, as defined by `ForeignKey.__init__()` in [django/db/models/fields/related.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/models/fields/related.py:962). Django also checks that `SET_NULL` fields are nullable in `ForeignKey._check_on_delete()` at [related.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/models/fields/related.py:1083).\n\nArchitectural implications of `SET_NULL` for both parents include:\n\n- Deleting a parent preserves every child record. Django\u2019s `SET_NULL()` schedules the affected FK column to be updated to `None`, rather than deleting descendants ([django/db/models/deletion.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:69)).\n- The family graph supports partial or unknown ancestry: either parental edge may be absent independently.\n- Deletion is non-propagating, avoiding potentially large recursive cascades through descendants, but permanently discards that genealogical edge. Historical parentage would require soft deletion or a separate relationship/history model.\n- Consumers must handle `None` at every traversal step; the model cannot assume every person has two reachable parents.\n- This is a general directed graph, not an enforced tree or DAG. There are no model constraints preventing self-parenting, cycles, or the same person being assigned to both fields. Recursive traversal therefore needs cycle detection or a depth limit.\n- Although the database fields accept `NULL`, neither parental field specifies `blank=True`; consequently, generated forms treat them as required unless customized, while ORM/database operations can still store `None`.\n\nThus, the design favors survivor-oriented, flexible graph relationships and referential validity over strict genealogical completeness, immutable lineage, or acyclic-tree enforcement.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 30974, "output_tokens": 1715, "total_tokens": 32689, "cache_read_tokens": 232448, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 7, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": [".\\\\django\\\\db\\\\models\\\\deletion.py", ".\\\\django\\\\db\\\\models\\\\fields\\\\related.py", ".\\\\tests\\\\m2o_recursive\\\\models.py", ".\\\\tests\\\\m2o_recursive\\\\tests.py", ".\\\\tests\\\\nested_foreign_keys\\\\models.py", "\\\\django\\\\db\\\\models\\\\deletion.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "c0-bare", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "c0-bare", "server_name": null, "launch_command": null, "launch_args": null, "served_tools": null, "client_tools": [], "index": null, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-c0-bare-django-django"}, "served_tools": [], "served_count": null, "mcp_tools_issued": [], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": null, "mcp_per_server": {}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 80.76334023475647, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 7.072261095046997, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.0849235, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T13:59:49.649808+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: What is the architectural pattern of the Person model's self-referential foreign key that enables recursive relationship traversal while maintaining data integrity constraints, and what architectural implications arise from using SET_NULL cascading behavior for both parental relationships?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 80762, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_006_c0-bare.json"}
{"task_id": "django_006", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "repowise", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The `Person` relationship uses Django\u2019s adjacency-list pattern: each person stores two nullable foreign keys back to the same `Person` model\u2014one per parent. Django represents this recursive target with `\"self\"` (`RECURSIVE_RELATIONSHIP_CONSTANT`) and resolves it through `resolve_relation()` in [related.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/models/fields/related.py). This permits repeated ORM traversal such as person \u2192 parent \u2192 grandparent while retaining database-enforced foreign-key validity.\n\nBoth parental fields use `on_delete=models.SET_NULL`, whose implementation in [deletion.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:69) schedules the referencing field to be updated to `None`:\n\n```python\ndef SET_NULL(collector, field, sub_objs, using):\n    collector.add_field_update(field, None, sub_objs)\n```\n\nArchitecturally, this means:\n\n- A parent\u2019s deletion does not cascade into deleting descendants.\n- Each parental link is independently cleared if it references the deleted person.\n- No dangling foreign keys remain, preserving referential integrity.\n- The family graph becomes deletion-tolerant but historically lossy: surviving people can become roots or have incomplete ancestry.\n- Traversal and business logic must treat both parents as optional and handle `None`.\n- Unless separate model constraints exist, the self-reference alone does not prevent cycles, self-parenting, or the same person occupying both parent roles. `SET_NULL` governs deletion behavior, not genealogical correctness.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 8, "relevance": 9, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 14716, "output_tokens": 604, "total_tokens": 15320, "cache_read_tokens": 41216, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 1, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": [], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer"], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "repowise", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "repowise", "server_name": "repowise", "launch_command": "C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/repowise-layerb/.venv/Scripts/repowise.exe", "launch_args": ["mcp", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-repowise-full-django-django", "--transport", "stdio"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer", "mcp__repowise__get_context", "mcp__repowise__get_overview", "mcp__repowise__get_risk", "mcp__repowise__get_symbol", "mcp__repowise__get_why", "mcp__repowise__search_codebase"], "index": null, "shares_index_with": "repowise-full", "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-repowise-full-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["get_answer", "get_change_risk", "get_context", "get_dead_code", "get_health", "get_overview", "get_risk", "get_symbol", "get_why", "list_repos", "search_codebase"], "served_count": 11, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"repowise": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"builtin": "repowise_legacy", "rc": 0, "seconds": 0.0, "index_vector_dim": 1536, "index_embedder_mock": false}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 25.519123315811157, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 5.975267648696899, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.029587000000000002, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:01:17.746362+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: What is the architectural pattern of the Person model's self-referential foreign key that enables recursive relationship traversal while maintaining data integrity constraints, and what architectural implications arise from using SET_NULL cascading behavior for both parental relationships?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 25517, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_006_repowise.json"}
{"task_id": "django_006", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "codegraph", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The `Person` model uses an adjacency-list pattern with two independent, directed self-referential foreign keys:\n\n- `mother` points from a child `Person` to another `Person`.\n- `father` does the same.\n- Distinct reverse names\u2014`mothers_child_set` and `fathers_child_set`\u2014disambiguate the two roles and support traversal in both directions.\n\nThis is defined in [tests/m2o_recursive/models.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/tests/m2o_recursive/models.py:26). Forward traversal can follow ancestry (`person.mother`, `person.father`, then recursively onward), while reverse managers find maternal or paternal descendants. `MultipleManyToOneRecursiveTests.test_m2o_recursive2()` demonstrates both directions in [tests/m2o_recursive/tests.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/tests/m2o_recursive/tests.py:33).\n\nData integrity comes from ordinary foreign-key constraints: each non-null parent ID must reference an existing `Person`. However, this model declares no check constraints preventing self-parenting, identical mother and father values, or longer ancestry cycles. Therefore, application-level recursive traversal must include cycle detection if arbitrary data is possible.\n\nBoth fields use `SET_NULL` and `null=True`. Django implements `SET_NULL` by scheduling the referencing field to be updated to `None` before deleting the referenced object in [django/db/models/deletion.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:69). Architecturally, this means:\n\n- Deleting a parent preserves all child records rather than cascading through the family graph.\n- Only the corresponding role is cleared: deleting a mother nulls `mother`; deleting a father nulls `father`.\n- If one person occupies both roles, both relationships can be cleared.\n- Referential integrity remains valid, but genealogical completeness does not: surviving people may acquire missing ancestry links.\n- Recursive queries must treat `NULL` as a traversal boundary.\n- Parent deletion has potentially broad update cost because every referencing child must be updated.\n- The graph favors entity-lifecycle independence and retention over immutable historical lineage. If lineage must survive deletion, soft deletion or a separate immutable relationship/history model would be more appropriate.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 8, "completeness": 9, "reasoning": 8, "relevance": 9, "clarity": 9}, "input_tokens": 34072, "output_tokens": 1479, "total_tokens": 35551, "cache_read_tokens": 194560, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 6, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["tests\\\\inline_formsets\\\\models.py", "tests\\\\m2o_recursive\\\\models.py", "tests\\\\m2o_recursive\\\\tests.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "codegraph", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "codegraph", "server_name": "codegraph", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "launch_args": ["serve", "--mcp", "--path", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django", "--no-watch"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "init", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["codegraph_explore"], "served_count": 1, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore", "mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"codegraph": {"ok": 2, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "codegraph", "rc": 0, "seconds": 2.6, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "init", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 69.24065947532654, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 5.5980284214019775, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.0817, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:01:55.641722+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: What is the architectural pattern of the Person model's self-referential foreign key that enables recursive relationship traversal while maintaining data integrity constraints, and what architectural implications arise from using SET_NULL cascading behavior for both parental relationships?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 69238, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_006_codegraph.json"}
{"task_id": "django_006", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "graphify", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The `Person` model uses a dual adjacency-list pattern: two nullable, self-referential many-to-one foreign keys represent role-specific edges in a family graph.\n\n- `mother = ForeignKey(\"self\", ...)`\n- `father = ForeignKey(\"self\", ...)`\n\nEach person can point upward to two other `Person` rows, while distinct reverse names\u2014`mothers_child_set` and `fathers_child_set`\u2014allow traversal downward by parental role. The behavior is demonstrated in [`tests/m2o_recursive/tests.py`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/tests/m2o_recursive/tests.py:21), including both child-to-parent and parent-to-children navigation.\n\nThe foreign keys provide database referential integrity: a non-null parent ID must identify an existing `Person`. At the same time, `null=True` supports root or unknown-parent records and is required for `SET_NULL`. The implementation of `SET_NULL` schedules the referencing field to be updated to `None` when the target is deleted ([`django/db/models/deletion.py`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:69)).\n\nArchitecturally, applying `SET_NULL` to both parental relationships means:\n\n- Deleting a parent does not recursively delete children or descendants; surviving records remain valid.\n- The corresponding lineage edge is severed, so ancestry information is intentionally lost unless preserved elsewhere.\n- A person referenced in both roles, or by many descendants, can be deleted without deletion-order problems; all affected references become null.\n- Recursive traversal must treat null as a normal termination condition and should still guard against cycles\u2014the model does not prevent someone from being their own ancestor.\n- The schema does not enforce domain rules such as different mother/father rows, parental roles, or acyclic lineage. Those require validation or additional constraints.\n\nThus, [`Person`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/tests/m2o_recursive/models.py:24) is a resilient, role-labeled recursive graph: it prioritizes preservation of person records and referential validity over preservation of complete historical lineage.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 17009, "output_tokens": 1384, "total_tokens": 18393, "cache_read_tokens": 147456, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 7, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": [".\\\\django\\\\db\\\\models\\\\deletion.py", ".\\\\tests\\\\m2o_recursive\\\\models.py", ".\\\\tests\\\\m2o_recursive\\\\tests.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "graphify", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "graphify", "server_name": "graphify", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify-mcp.exe", "launch_args": ["--transport", "stdio", "--graph", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django/graphify-out/graph.json"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__graphify__query_graph", "mcp__graphify__get_node", "mcp__graphify__get_neighbors", "mcp__graphify__shortest_path", "mcp__graphify__get_community", "mcp__graphify__god_nodes", "mcp__graphify__graph_stats"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify.exe", "update", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["get_community", "get_neighbors", "get_node", "get_pr_impact", "god_nodes", "graph_stats", "list_prs", "query_graph", "shortest_path", "triage_prs"], "served_count": 10, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__graphify__query_graph", "mcp__graphify__query_graph"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"graphify": {"ok": 2, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "graphify", "rc": 0, "seconds": 167.4, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify.exe", "update", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 77.34028029441833, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 5.044241189956665, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.05353325, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:03:15.747948+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: What is the architectural pattern of the Person model's self-referential foreign key that enables recursive relationship traversal while maintaining data integrity constraints, and what architectural implications arise from using SET_NULL cascading behavior for both parental relationships?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 77338, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_006_graphify.json"}
{"task_id": "django_006", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "code-review-graph", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The `Person` model uses a **self-referential adjacency-list pattern**: each row has two nullable foreign keys\u2014`mother` and `father`\u2014pointing back to another row in the same table via `\"self\"` ([models.py](/C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/tests/m2o_recursive/models.py:26)).\n\nKey characteristics:\n\n- Each person has up to two typed, directed parent edges.\n- Traversal works forward through `person.mother` and `person.father`.\n- Distinct reverse managers\u2014`mothers_child_set` and `fathers_child_set`\u2014allow traversal from a parent to children without conflating maternal and paternal roles ([models.py](/C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/tests/m2o_recursive/models.py:28)).\n- The tests demonstrate both directions of traversal ([tests.py](/C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/tests/m2o_recursive/tests.py:33)).\n- Database foreign keys prevent references to nonexistent people, while `null=True` permits unknown or absent parents. Django explicitly rejects `SET_NULL` on non-nullable fields with `fields.E320` ([related.py](/C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/fields/related.py:1088)).\n\nUsing `SET_NULL` for both relationships means deleting a parent does **not** recursively delete their descendants. Django\u2019s deletion collector updates the affected foreign-key columns to `NULL` ([deletion.py](/C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:69)). Architecturally, this:\n\n- Preserves descendant records and the rest of the family graph.\n- Avoids destructive cascade chains across generations.\n- Maintains referential integrity\u2014there are no dangling foreign-key IDs.\n- Sacrifices lineage information when a parent is deleted; descendants become roots or partially connected nodes.\n- Treats maternal and paternal links independently, so deleting one parent leaves the other relationship intact.\n- Requires application-level archival or history storage if deleted ancestry must remain auditable.\n\nThe model enforces reference validity, but it does not itself prevent cycles, self-parenting, inconsistent generations, or semantic misuse of the mother/father roles; those constraints would require additional validation or database constraints.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 8, "relevance": 9, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 19733, "output_tokens": 1568, "total_tokens": 21301, "cache_read_tokens": 131328, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 8, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django\\\\db\\\\models\\\\deletion.py", "django\\\\db\\\\models\\\\fields\\\\related.py", "tests\\\\m2o_recursive\\\\models.py", "tests\\\\m2o_recursive\\\\tests.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "code-review-graph", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "code-review-graph", "server_name": "crg", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "launch_args": ["serve", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__crg__semantic_search_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__query_graph_tool", "mcp__crg__traverse_graph_tool", "mcp__crg__get_flow_tool", "mcp__crg__list_flows_tool", "mcp__crg__get_affected_flows_tool", "mcp__crg__get_architecture_overview_tool", "mcp__crg__get_bridge_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__get_community_tool", "mcp__crg__list_communities_tool", "mcp__crg__get_hub_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__get_impact_radius_tool", "mcp__crg__get_knowledge_gaps_tool", "mcp__crg__get_minimal_context_tool", "mcp__crg__get_docs_section_tool", "mcp__crg__get_wiki_page_tool", "mcp__crg__get_suggested_questions_tool", "mcp__crg__get_surprising_connections_tool", "mcp__crg__find_large_functions_tool", "mcp__crg__list_graph_stats_tool"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "build", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django", "--embedding-provider", "local", "--embedding-model", "sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["apply_refactor_tool", "build_or_update_graph_tool", "cross_repo_search_tool", "detect_changes_tool", "embed_graph_tool", "find_large_functions_tool", "generate_wiki_tool", "get_affected_flows_tool", "get_architecture_overview_tool", "get_bridge_nodes_tool", "get_community_tool", "get_docs_section_tool", "get_flow_tool", "get_hub_nodes_tool", "get_impact_radius_tool", "get_knowledge_gaps_tool", "get_minimal_context_tool", "get_review_context_tool", "get_suggested_questions_tool", "get_surprising_connections_tool", "get_wiki_page_tool", "list_communities_tool", "list_flows_tool", "list_graph_stats_tool", "list_repos_tool", "query_graph_tool", "refactor_tool", "run_postprocess_tool", "semantic_search_nodes_tool", "traverse_graph_tool"], "served_count": 30, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__crg__semantic_search_nodes_tool"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"crg": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "code-review-graph", "rc": 0, "seconds": 160.3, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "build", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django", "--embedding-provider", "local", "--embedding-model", "sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 70.77855205535889, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 5.2503485679626465, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.05676225, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:04:43.009279+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: What is the architectural pattern of the Person model's self-referential foreign key that enables recursive relationship traversal while maintaining data integrity constraints, and what architectural implications arise from using SET_NULL cascading behavior for both parental relationships?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 70776, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_006_code-review-graph.json"}
{"task_id": "django_006", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "serena", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The `Person` model uses an adjacency-list pattern with two role-specific, self-referential many-to-one relationships:\n\n- `mother = ForeignKey(\"self\", ...)`\n- `father = ForeignKey(\"self\", ...)`\n\nIn [tests/m2o_recursive/models.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/tests/m2o_recursive/models.py:26), each `Person` row is a node, while `mother_id` and `father_id` are directed edges to other rows in the same table. This enables recursive traversal:\n\n- Upward: `person.mother`, `person.father`, `person.mother.mother`, etc.\n- Downward: the distinct reverse managers `mothers_child_set` and `fathers_child_set`.\n\nSeparate `related_name` values are architecturally important because two foreign keys target the same model; they preserve the semantic role of each reverse relationship and avoid accessor collisions.\n\nData integrity comes from ordinary foreign-key constraints: a parent reference must point to an existing `Person` or be `NULL`. Both fields specify `null=True`, which is required for `SET_NULL`; Django explicitly checks this compatibility in [django/db/models/fields/related.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/fields/related.py:1088).\n\nThe implications of `SET_NULL` for both relationships are:\n\n- Deleting a parent does not recursively delete children or descendants. Django\u2019s `SET_NULL()` implementation schedules the referencing field to be updated to `None` in [django/db/models/deletion.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:69).\n- A deleted mother clears only matching `mother_id` edges; a deleted father clears matching `father_id` edges. The two parental roles remain independent.\n- Person records survive ancestry deletion, avoiding destructive deletion of an entire family graph.\n- The tradeoff is loss of lineage information: surviving people may have incomplete ancestry and effectively become roots of disconnected subgraphs.\n- Recursive algorithms must treat either parent as an optional termination point.\n- The model guarantees referential integrity, but not graph integrity: it does not itself prevent self-parenting, cycles, the same person being both parents, or biologically impossible relationships. Those require validation or additional database constraints.\n\nSo this is a resilient, nullable adjacency-list graph: identity and descendant records are prioritized over preserving every historical relationship edge.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 63233, "output_tokens": 1690, "total_tokens": 64923, "cache_read_tokens": 246272, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 7, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": [], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "serena", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "serena", "server_name": "serena", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/serena.exe", "launch_args": ["start-mcp-server", "--project", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-serena-django-django", "--transport", "stdio", "--enable-web-dashboard", "false", "--enable-gui-log-window", "false"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__get_symbols_overview", "mcp__serena__find_declaration", "mcp__serena__find_implementations", "mcp__serena__find_referencing_symbols", "mcp__serena__get_diagnostics_for_file", "mcp__serena__find_file", "mcp__serena__list_dir", "mcp__serena__read_file"], "index": null, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-serena-django-django"}