ut": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:40:58.757627+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: How does the OFTInteger class coordinate the _bit64 attribute with the as_int() method to handle the semantic mismatch between GDAL's internal OFTReal representation and the exposed OFTInteger type?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 76315, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_014_serena.json"}
{"task_id": "django_017", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "c0-bare", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "`SomeClass.method5()` deliberately raises a plain `TypeError` from inside its body ([tests/template_tests/utils.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/tests/template_tests/utils.py:118)). Django distinguishes that from a `TypeError` caused by calling a method that requires arguments.\n\nFor `{{ var.method5 }}`, the execution path is:\n\n1. `Template.render()` invokes `_render()`, which renders its `NodeList` ([django/template/base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/template/base.py:165)).\n2. `NodeList.render()` calls `render_annotated()` on the `VariableNode` ([django/template/base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/template/base.py:1090)).\n3. `VariableNode.render()` calls `FilterExpression.resolve()` ([django/template/base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/template/base.py:1148)).\n4. `FilterExpression.resolve()` calls `Variable.resolve()`, which reaches `_resolve_lookup()` ([django/template/base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/template/base.py:785)).\n5. `_resolve_lookup()` retrieves the bound `method5` and automatically calls it because it is callable ([django/template/base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/template/base.py:993)).\n\nWhen that call raises `TypeError`, `_resolve_lookup()` inspects the callable\u2019s signature and calls `signature.bind()` with no arguments ([django/template/base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/template/base.py:1001)). Because bound `method5()` requires no arguments, binding succeeds. Django therefore concludes that the `TypeError` originated inside the method, rather than from an invalid template invocation, and re-raises the original exception at line 1013.\n\nThe outer resolution handler logs the failure at debug level, but only converts exceptions with `silent_variable_failure = True` into `string_if_invalid` ([django/template/base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/template/base.py:1014)). Plain `TypeError` has no such attribute, so it is re-raised. `VariableNode.render()` only suppresses `UnicodeDecodeError`, not `TypeError`.\n\nFinally, `Node.render_annotated()` may attach template debugging information\u2014such as the culprit node and source location\u2014when template debugging is enabled, but it then re-raises the exception ([django/template/base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/template/base.py:1044)). Consequently, rendering aborts and the `TypeError` propagates out through `NodeList.render()` and `Template.render()` to the caller.\n\nThis behavior is explicitly verified by `test_basic_syntax20b`, which expects `template.render(...)` to raise `TypeError` ([tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_basic.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_basic.py:180)). It contrasts with methods that merely require arguments, which Django treats as invalid template calls and renders as `string_if_invalid`.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 13866, "output_tokens": 1535, "total_tokens": 15401, "cache_read_tokens": 89600, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 5, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/template/base.py", "tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_basic.py", "tests/template_tests/utils.py", "utils.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "c0-bare", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "c0-bare", "server_name": null, "launch_command": null, "launch_args": null, "served_tools": null, "client_tools": [], "index": null, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-c0-bare-django-django"}, "served_tools": [], "served_count": null, "mcp_tools_issued": [], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": null, "mcp_per_server": {}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 56.03791260719299, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 7.914290189743042, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.043882500000000005, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:42:34.889711+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: How does the exception-raising behavior of method5 in SomeClass interact with Django's template rendering framework to affect error handling and propagation through the template engine's execution stack?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 56036, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_017_c0-bare.json"}
{"task_id": "django_017", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "repowise", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "`SomeClass.method5()` deliberately raises a bare `TypeError` in [tests/template_tests/utils.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/tests/template_tests/utils.py:118). When a template evaluates `{{ var.method5 }}`, Django handles it as follows:\n\n1. `Variable._resolve_lookup()` finds the bound method and automatically invokes it because it is callable ([django/template/base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/template/base.py:948)).\n2. The invocation raises `TypeError`. Django must distinguish an exception raised *inside* a valid zero-argument method from the normal `TypeError` produced when a callable requires arguments.\n3. It inspects the callable\u2019s signature and calls `current_signature.bind()` ([django/template/base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/template/base.py:999)). For `method5`, binding with no arguments succeeds, proving that the call itself was valid. Django therefore re-raises the original `TypeError` at line 1013. If binding failed, Django would instead substitute `string_if_invalid`.\n4. The outer resolver catches the exception only to log it. Because `TypeError` does not have `silent_variable_failure=True`, it is re-raised again rather than converted into an invalid-variable value ([django/template/base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/template/base.py:1014)).\n5. `VariableNode.render()` does not catch `TypeError`, so it escapes into `Node.render_annotated()` ([django/template/base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/template/base.py:1148)). With template debugging enabled, that method adds `_culprit_node` and `template_debug` location data, but then re-raises the same exception.\n6. It consequently unwinds through `NodeList.render()`, `Template._render()`, and `Template.render()` rather than producing partial or empty template output.\n\nThis behavior is explicitly asserted by `test_basic_syntax20b()` in [tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_basic.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_basic.py:180): rendering `{{ var.method5 }}` must raise `TypeError`.\n\nThus, the template engine annotates the error in debug mode but neither wraps nor suppresses it. During an HTTP request, if nothing closer catches it, it continues beyond template rendering into Django\u2019s request exception handling and is treated as an uncaught server error\u2014normally becoming a 500 response, or propagating further when `DEBUG_PROPAGATE_EXCEPTIONS` is enabled.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 34306, "output_tokens": 1237, "total_tokens": 35543, "cache_read_tokens": 147968, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 4, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/template/base.py", "tests/template_tests/utils.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer"], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "repowise", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "repowise", "server_name": "repowise", "launch_command": "C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/repowise-layerb/.venv/Scripts/repowise.exe", "launch_args": ["mcp", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-repowise-full-django-django", "--transport", "stdio"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer", "mcp__repowise__get_context", "mcp__repowise__get_overview", "mcp__repowise__get_risk", "mcp__repowise__get_symbol", "mcp__repowise__get_why", "mcp__repowise__search_codebase"], "index": null, "shares_index_with": "repowise-full", "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-repowise-full-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["get_answer", "get_change_risk", "get_context", "get_dead_code", "get_health", "get_overview", "get_risk", "get_symbol", "get_why", "list_repos", "search_codebase"], "served_count": 11, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"repowise": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"builtin": "repowise_legacy", "rc": 0, "seconds": 0.0, "index_vector_dim": 1536, "index_embedder_mock": false}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 48.68062448501587, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 7.983642101287842, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.0737485, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:43:38.968692+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: How does the exception-raising behavior of method5 in SomeClass interact with Django's template rendering framework to affect error handling and propagation through the template engine's execution stack?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 48678, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_017_repowise.json"}
{"task_id": "django_017", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "codegraph", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "`SomeClass.method5()` is deliberately a zero-argument callable that raises `TypeError` from inside its body:\n\n- [`tests/template_tests/utils.py:102`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/tests/template_tests/utils.py:102)\n- [`tests/template_tests/utils.py:118`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/tests/template_tests/utils.py:118)\n\nWhen rendering `{{ var.method5 }}`, the execution path is:\n\n`Template.render()` \u2192 `Template._render()` \u2192 `NodeList.render()` \u2192 `VariableNode.render()` \u2192 filter/variable resolution \u2192 `Variable._resolve_lookup()` \u2192 `SomeClass.method5()`.\n\nThe important behavior occurs in [`Variable._resolve_lookup()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/template/base.py:948):\n\n1. Django finds the bound `method5` attribute and sees that it is callable.\n2. It invokes it with `current = current()` at line 1000.\n3. That invocation raises `TypeError`.\n4. Because `TypeError` can also mean \u201cthis callable requires arguments,\u201d Django inspects the callable\u2019s signature and tries `current_signature.bind()`.\n5. `method5` requires no arguments, so binding succeeds. Django therefore knows that the original `TypeError` came from inside the method, rather than from calling it incorrectly, and re-raises it at line 1013.\n\nThe outer resolver handler logs the resolution failure at debug level. It only converts exceptions carrying `silent_variable_failure = True` into `string_if_invalid`; an ordinary `TypeError` has no such attribute, so it is re-raised at [`django/template/base.py:1023`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/template/base.py:1023).\n\nPropagation then continues as follows:\n\n- `VariableNode.render()` only suppresses `UnicodeDecodeError`, not `TypeError` ([`django/template/base.py:1148`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/template/base.py:1148)).\n- `Node.render_annotated()` catches the exception only to attach the culprit node and `template_debug` source-location information when template debugging is enabled, then re-raises it ([`django/template/base.py:1044`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/template/base.py:1044)).\n- `NodeList.render()`, `Template._render()`, and `Template.render()` do not suppress it ([`django/template/base.py:1090`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/template/base.py:1090), [`django/template/base.py:165`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/template/base.py:165)).\n- The Django template backend catches only `TemplateDoesNotExist`, so a `TypeError` also passes through it unchanged ([`django/template/backends/django.py:102`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/template/backends/django.py:102)).\n\nThus, `method5` demonstrates that Django does not mistake an internal `TypeError` for an invalid template method call and silently replace it with an empty/invalid value. The exception remains a `TypeError`, may gain template-debug metadata, and escapes the complete template-rendering stack to the caller. The corresponding test explicitly asserts this behavior at [`tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_basic.py:180`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_basic.py:180).", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 22781, "output_tokens": 1857, "total_tokens": 24638, "cache_read_tokens": 187136, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 7, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/template/backends/django.py", "django/template/base.py", "django/template/engine.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "codegraph", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "codegraph", "server_name": "codegraph", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "launch_args": ["serve", "--mcp", "--path", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django", "--no-watch"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "init", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["codegraph_explore"], "served_count": 1, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"codegraph": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "codegraph", "rc": 0, "seconds": 1.1, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "init", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 81.57969498634338, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 8.731812477111816, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.07043825000000001, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:44:42.145584+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: How does the exception-raising behavior of method5 in SomeClass interact with Django's template rendering framework to affect error handling and propagation through the template engine's execution stack?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 81577, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_017_codegraph.json"}
{"task_id": "django_017", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "graphify", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "`SomeClass.method5()` deliberately raises a bare `TypeError` in [tests/template_tests/utils.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/tests/template_tests/utils.py:118). Django treats this differently from a callable that merely requires arguments.\n\nFor `{{ var.method5 }}`, the execution path is:\n\n`Template.render()` \u2192 `Template._render()` \u2192 `NodeList.render()` \u2192 `VariableNode.render()` \u2192 `FilterExpression.resolve()` \u2192 `Variable.resolve()` \u2192 `Variable._resolve_lookup()` \u2192 `SomeClass.method5()`\n\nThe key behavior is in [`Variable._resolve_lookup()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/template/base.py:948):\n\n- Django automatically invokes resolved callable attributes with `current()`.\n- When that invocation raises `TypeError`, Django inspects the callable\u2019s signature.\n- If binding an empty argument list fails, Django concludes that required arguments were omitted and substitutes `engine.string_if_invalid`.\n- `method5` accepts no arguments, so empty signature binding succeeds. Django therefore knows the `TypeError` originated inside the method rather than from an invalid template invocation, and re-raises it at line 1013.\n\nThe outer exception handler in `_resolve_lookup()` logs the resolution failure but suppresses it only when the exception has `silent_variable_failure=True`. The plain `TypeError` from `method5` does not, so it is raised again at line 1026.\n\nAbove that point:\n\n- [`VariableNode.render()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/template/base.py:1148) catches only `UnicodeDecodeError`, so the `TypeError` passes through.\n- [`Node.render_annotated()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/template/base.py:1044) catches it temporarily. In debug mode it attaches the culprit node and template source information (`_culprit_node` and `template_debug`), then re-raises the same exception.\n- [`NodeList.render()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/template/base.py:1090) and [`Template.render()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/template/base.py:168) do not suppress it, so it escapes the template engine to the caller.\n\nThis contract is explicitly tested by `test_basic_syntax20b()` in [tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_basic.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_basic.py:180), which asserts that rendering `{{ var.method5 }}` raises `TypeError`. Thus, Django avoids mistaking application bugs inside template-callable methods for harmless argument mismatches, while still enriching the propagated error with template context when debugging is enabled.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 8, "completeness": 8, "relevance": 9, "clarity": 8, "reasoning": 8}, "input_tokens": 21980, "output_tokens": 1726, "total_tokens": 23706, "cache_read_tokens": 163584, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 7, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/template/base.py", "django/template/engine.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "graphify", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "graphify", "server_name": "graphify", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify-mcp.exe", "launch_args": ["--transport", "stdio", "--graph", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django/graphify-out/graph.json"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__graphify__query_graph", "mcp__graphify__get_node", "mcp__graphify__get_neighbors", "mcp__graphify__shortest_path", "mcp__graphify__get_community", "mcp__graphify__god_nodes", "mcp__graphify__graph_stats"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify.exe", "update", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["get_community", "get_neighbors", "get_node", "get_pr_impact", "god_nodes", "graph_stats", "list_prs", "query_graph", "shortest_path", "triage_prs"], "served_count": 10, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__graphify__query_graph"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"graphify": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "graphify", "rc": 0, "seconds": 211.2, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify.exe", "update", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 79.53664755821228, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 9.96330976486206, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.065183, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:46:13.853293+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: How does the exception-raising behavior of method5 in SomeClass interact with Django's template rendering framework to affect error handling and propagation through the template engine's execution stack?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 79534, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_017_graphify.json"}
{"task_id": "django_017", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "code-review-graph", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "`SomeClass.method5()` deliberately raises a bare `TypeError` ([tests/template_tests/utils.py:118](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/tests/template_tests/utils.py:118)). Django distinguishes this from a `TypeError` caused by calling a method with missing arguments.\n\nFor `{{ var.method5 }}`, execution proceeds as follows:\n\n1. `Template.render()` delegates through `_render()` to `NodeList.render()` ([django/template/base.py:168](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/template/base.py:168)).\n2. `NodeList.render()` invokes `VariableNode.render_annotated()` ([django/template/base.py:1090](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/template/base.py:1090)).\n3. `VariableNode.render()` calls `FilterExpression.resolve()`, then `Variable.resolve()` and `_resolve_lookup()` ([django/template/base.py:1148](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/template/base.py:1148)).\n4. `_resolve_lookup()` resolves `var.method5` as a callable and invokes it with no arguments ([django/template/base.py:993](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/template/base.py:993)).\n5. When `method5()` raises `TypeError`, Django inspects its signature and calls `current_signature.bind()`:\n\n   - If binding fails, the callable actually required arguments, so Django treats it as an invalid template invocation and substitutes `string_if_invalid`.\n   - Here binding succeeds because `method5(self)` needs no additional bound arguments. Therefore the `TypeError` originated inside the method, and the bare `raise` at line 1013 re-raises it.\n\nThe outer exception handler in `_resolve_lookup()` logs the resolution failure. It suppresses only exceptions having `silent_variable_failure=True`; a normal `TypeError` does not, so it is re-raised again ([django/template/base.py:1014](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/template/base.py:1014)).\n\nAs the exception unwinds, `Node.render_annotated()` may attach the culprit node and template source information when template debugging is enabled, but it always re-raises the exception ([django/template/base.py:1044](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/template/base.py:1044)). Neither `VariableNode.render()`, `NodeList.render()`, nor `Template.render()` consumes it, so the original `TypeError` reaches the caller.\n\nThis behavior is explicitly verified by `test_basic_syntax20b()`, which expects rendering `{{ var.method5 }}` to raise `TypeError` ([tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_basic.py:180](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_basic.py:180)).", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 8, "completeness": 8, "relevance": 9, "clarity": 8, "reasoning": 8}, "input_tokens": 24259, "output_tokens": 1538, "total_tokens": 25797, "cache_read_tokens": 136192, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 6, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/template/base.py", "tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_basic.py", "tests/template_tests/utils.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "code-review-graph", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "code-review-graph", "server_name": "crg", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "launch_args": ["serve", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__crg__semantic_search_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__query_graph_tool", "mcp__crg__traverse_graph_tool", "mcp__crg__get_flow_tool", "mcp__crg__list_flows_tool", "mcp__crg__get_affected_flows_tool", "mcp__crg__get_architecture_overview_tool", "mcp__crg__get_bridge_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__get_community_tool", "mcp__crg__list_communities_tool", "mcp__crg__get_hub_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__get_impact_radius_tool", "mcp__crg__get_knowledge_gaps_tool", "mcp__crg__get_minimal_context_tool", "mcp__crg__get_docs_section_tool", "mcp__crg__get_wiki_page_tool", "mcp__crg__get_suggested_questions_tool", "mcp__crg__get_surprising_connections_tool", "mcp__crg__find_large_functions_tool", "mcp__crg__list_graph_stats_tool"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "build", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django", "--embedding-provider", "local", "--embedding-model", "sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["apply_refactor_tool", "build_or_update_graph_tool", "cross_repo_search_tool", "detect_changes_tool", "embed_graph_tool", "find_large_functions_tool", "generate_wiki_tool", "get_affected_flows_tool", "get_architecture_overview_tool", "get_bridge_nodes_tool", "get_community_tool", "get_docs_section_tool", "get_flow_tool", "get_hub_nodes_tool", "get_impact_radius_tool", "get_knowledge_gaps_tool", "get_minimal_context_tool", "get_review_context_tool", "get_suggested_questions_tool", "get_surprising_connections_tool", "get_wiki_page_tool", "list_communities_tool", "list_flows_tool", "list_graph_stats_tool", "list_repos_tool", "query_graph_tool", "refactor_tool", "run_postprocess_tool", "semantic_search_nodes_tool", "traverse_graph_tool"], "served_count": 30, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__crg__semantic_search_nodes_tool"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"crg": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "code-review-graph", "rc": 0, "seconds": 259.0, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "build", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django", "--embedding-provider", "local", "--embedding-model", "sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 76.96401619911194, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 8.405462503433228, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.06272775, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:47:48.464135+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: How does the exception-raising behavior of method5 in SomeClass interact with Django's template rendering framework to affect error handling and propagation through the template engine's execution stack?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 76961, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_017_code-review-graph.json"}
{"task_id": "django_017", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "serena", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "`SomeClass.method5()` is deliberately designed to verify that Django does not mistake a `TypeError` raised *inside* a callable for a template lookup or argument-binding failure:\n\n```python\ndef method5(self):\n    raise TypeError\n```\n\nIt is defined in [tests/template_tests/utils.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/tests/template_tests/utils.py:118) and exercised by `test_basic_syntax20b()` in [tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_basic.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_basic.py:180), which renders `{{ var.method5 }}` and asserts that `TypeError` escapes.\n\nThe execution path is:\n\n`Template.render()` \u2192 `Template._render()` \u2192 `NodeList.render()` \u2192 `Node.render_annotated()` \u2192 `VariableNode.render()` \u2192 `FilterExpression.resolve()` \u2192 `Variable.resolve()` \u2192 `Variable._resolve_lookup()` \u2192 `SomeClass.method5()`.\n\nThe key logic is in `Variable._resolve_lookup()` in [django/template/base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/template/base.py:948):\n\n1. Django resolves `var.method5` to the bound method.\n2. Because it is callable, Django invokes it with no arguments.\n3. `method5()` raises `TypeError`.\n4. Django catches that `TypeError` temporarily because the same exception can mean that a callable requires arguments.\n5. It inspects the callable with `inspect.signature()` and calls `signature.bind()` with no arguments.\n6. Binding succeeds because `method5()` requires no unbound arguments. Therefore, the original `TypeError` came from inside the method, not from calling it incorrectly, so Django uses bare `raise` to propagate it.\n\nThe surrounding catch-all in `_resolve_lookup()` logs the resolution failure at debug level. It substitutes `string_if_invalid` only when the exception has `silent_variable_failure=True`; an ordinary `TypeError` does not, so it is re-raised.\n\nNothing higher in the normal rendering stack suppresses it:\n\n- `FilterExpression.resolve()` only converts `VariableDoesNotExist` into `string_if_invalid`.\n- `VariableNode.render()` only suppresses `UnicodeDecodeError`.\n- `Node.render_annotated()` catches general exceptions, optionally adds `_culprit_node` and `template_debug` information when template debugging is enabled, and then re-raises.\n- `NodeList.render()` and the core `Template.render()` do not catch it.\n\nThus, the caller ultimately receives the original `TypeError`. In debug mode it may carry template-location metadata, but its type and propagation are preserved. This distinguishes a genuine application error inside `method5()` from a harmless, non-callable-in-template method requiring arguments, which Django would render as `string_if_invalid`.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 27952, "output_tokens": 1224, "total_tokens": 29176, "cache_read_tokens": 148224, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 12, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": [], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "serena", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "serena", "server_name": "serena", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/serena.exe", "launch_args": ["start-mcp-server", "--project", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-serena-django-django", "--transport", "stdio", "--enable-web-dashboard", "false", "--enable-gui-log-window", "false"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__get_symbols_overview", "mcp__serena__find_declaration", "mcp__serena__find_implementations", "mcp__serena__find_referencing_symbols", "mcp__serena__get_diagnostics_for_file", "mcp__serena__find_file", "mcp__serena__list_dir", "mcp__serena__read_file"], "index": null, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-serena-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["activate_project", "create_text_file", "delete_memory", "edit_memory", "execute_shell_command", "find_declaration", "find_file", "find_implementations", "find_referencing_symbols", "find_symbol", "get_current_config", "get_diagnostics_for_file", "get_symbols_overview", "initial_instructions", "insert_after_symbol", "insert_before_symbol", "list_dir", "list_memories", "onboarding", "read_file", "read_memory", "rename_memory", "rename_symbol", "replace_content", "replace_in_files", "replace_symbol_body", "safe_delete_symbol", "search_for_pattern", "write_memory"], "served_count": 29, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__serena__initial_instructions", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_referencing_symbols", "mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"serena": {"ok": 12, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"skipped": "no-index-by-design", "seconds": 0.0}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 72.15944457054138, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 7.760445594787598, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.065708, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:49:32.070995+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: How does the exception-raising behavior of method5 in SomeClass interact with Django's template rendering framework to affect error handling and propagation through the template engine's execution stack?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 72157, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_017_serena.json"}
{"task_id": "django_020", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "c0-bare", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "`ValidationError.messages` acts as a format-neutral projection over Django\u2019s two internal error representations:\n\n- Field-oriented errors are stored in `self.error_dict`.\n- Non-field or flat errors are stored in `self.error_list`.\n\nIn [`django/core/exceptions.py:199`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/core/exceptions.py:199), the property handles both through the class\u2019s iterator protocol:\n\n```python\n@property\ndef messages(self):\n    if hasattr(self, \"error_dict\"):\n        return sum(dict(self).values(), [])\n    return list(self)\n```\n\nThe architectural consistency comes from using `dict(self)` and `list(self)`, both of which delegate message rendering to `ValidationError.__iter__()` at [`django/core/exceptions.py:212`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/core/exceptions.py:212):\n\n- For `error_dict`, `__iter__()` yields `(field, rendered_messages)` pairs.\n- For `error_list`, it yields rendered strings, including parameter interpolation and `str()` conversion.\n\nThus, formatting logic lives in one place. `messages` does not separately reimplement traversal, interpolation, or string conversion for each representation. Its dictionary branch merely removes field grouping by flattening the already-rendered dictionary values with `sum(..., [])`; its list branch directly materializes the same iterator.\n\nNormalization happens earlier in `__init__()` at [`django/core/exceptions.py:166`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/core/exceptions.py:166), where dictionary values and nested lists become canonical lists of `ValidationError` objects. Consequently, the aggregation layer operates on normalized data rather than recursively inspecting heterogeneous inputs again.\n\nThis separation is also visible in `update_error_dict()` at [`django/core/exceptions.py:204`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/core/exceptions.py:204): it extends the destination directly from `error_dict` or `error_list`, preserving structured `ValidationError` objects and avoiding a render-and-reparse pass through `.messages`. Flat errors are consistently assigned to `NON_FIELD_ERRORS`.\n\nThe expected flattening behavior is explicitly tested in [`tests/test_exceptions/test_validation_error.py:8`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/tests/test_exceptions/test_validation_error.py:8), where messages from multiple fields are returned as one list.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 24103, "output_tokens": 1535, "total_tokens": 25638, "cache_read_tokens": 100096, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 7, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django\\\\core\\\\exceptions.py", "tests\\\\forms_tests\\\\tests\\\\test_forms.py", "tests\\\\test_exceptions\\\\test_validation_error.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "c0-bare", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "c0-bare", "server_name": null, "launch_command": null, "launch_args": null, "served_tools": null, "client_tools": [], "index": null, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-c0-bare-django-django"}, "served_tools": [], "served_count": null, "mcp_tools_issued": [], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": null, "mcp_per_server": {}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 57.36409378051758, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 16.286763906478882, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.05799075, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:51:03.005901+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: How does the ValidationError.messages property maintain architectural consistency between two distinct error representation formats while avoiding redundant data traversal in the aggregation layer?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 57362, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_020_c0-bare.json"}
{"task_id": "django_020", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "repowise", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "`ValidationError` normalizes errors into one of two internal representations in [`ValidationError.__init__`](</C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/core/exceptions.py:147>):\n\n- `error_dict`: field names mapped to lists of `ValidationError` objects.\n- `error_list`: a flat list of `ValidationError` objects.\n\nThe [`messages` property`](</C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/core/exceptions.py:199>) hides that distinction and always exposes a flat list of rendered strings:\n\n```python\nif hasattr(self, \"error_dict\"):\n    return sum(dict(self).values(), [])\nreturn list(self)\n```\n\nBoth branches reuse [`ValidationError.__iter__`](</C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/core/exceptions.py:212>) as the single rendering path, including parameter interpolation and string conversion. Dict-backed errors are rendered per field and flattened; list-backed errors are rendered directly. This keeps formatting behavior consistent without duplicating traversal and rendering logic inside `messages`.\n\nMeanwhile, [`update_error_dict()`](</C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/core/exceptions.py:204>)\u2014the aggregation layer\u2014does not call `messages` or iterate to render strings. It extends the destination directly from the stored `error_dict` or `error_list`, preserving structured `ValidationError` objects and their metadata while avoiding an unnecessary render/flatten/re-wrap traversal. Thus, presentation is centralized in `__iter__`, while structural aggregation operates directly on normalized data.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 8, "relevance": 9, "clarity": 8, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 12222, "output_tokens": 625, "total_tokens": 12847, "cache_read_tokens": 38144, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 1, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": [], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer"], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "repowise", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "repowise", "server_name": "repowise", "launch_command": "C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/repowise-layerb/.venv/Scripts/repowise.exe", "launch_args": ["mcp", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-repowise-full-django-django", "--transport", "stdio"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer", "mcp__repowise__get_context", "mcp__repowise__get_overview", "mcp__repowise__get_risk", "mcp__repowise__get_symbol", "mcp__repowise__get_why", "mcp__repowise__search_codebase"], "index": null, "shares_index_with": "repowise-full", "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-repowise-full-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["get_answer", "get_change_risk", "get_context", "get_dead_code", "get_health", "get_overview", "get_risk", "get_symbol", "get_why", "list_repos", "search_codebase"], "served_count": 11, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"repowise": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"builtin": "repowise_legacy", "rc": 0, "seconds": 0.0, "index_vector_dim": 1536, "index_embedder_mock": false}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 22.79506015777588, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 11.330405950546265, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.0262955, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:52:16.791914+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: How does the ValidationError.messages property maintain architectural consistency between two distinct error representation formats while avoiding redundant data traversal in the aggregation layer?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 22793, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_020_repowise.json"}
{"task_id": "django_020", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "codegraph", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "In [`django/core/exceptions.py`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/core/exceptions.py:190), `ValidationError` supports two normalized internal representations:\n\n- `error_dict`: field names mapped to lists of errors.\n- `error_list`: a flat list of errors.\n\nThe `messages` property preserves consistency between them by using `ValidationError.__iter__()` as the single formatting boundary:\n\n```python\n@property\ndef messages(self):\n    if hasattr(self, \"error_dict\"):\n        return sum(dict(self).values(), [])\n    return list(self)\n```\n\nFor dictionary errors, `dict(self)` invokes `__iter__()`, which yields each field alongside its formatted message list. `messages` then discards the field keys and flattens those lists. This is structurally consistent with `message_dict`, which also returns `dict(self)`.\n\nFor list errors, `list(self)` invokes the other branch of the same iterator, formatting each error\u2014including parameter interpolation\u2014into a string.\n\nThis design avoids redundant traversal because aggregation does not independently inspect or recursively format `error_dict` and `error_list`. Construction has already normalized nested errors in `ValidationError.__init__()` (lines 166\u2013188), and `__iter__()` performs the public-facing conversion once. The `messages` layer merely selects the appropriate iterator materialization and, for dictionaries, performs a shallow flattening of the already-produced lists. Thus both public formats share one serialization path without duplicating nested-error walking or message-formatting logic.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 9774, "output_tokens": 734, "total_tokens": 10508, "cache_read_tokens": 40192, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 1, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": [], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "codegraph", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "codegraph", "server_name": "codegraph", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "launch_args": ["serve", "--mcp", "--path", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django", "--no-watch"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "init", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["codegraph_explore"], "served_count": 1, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"codegraph": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "codegraph", "rc": 0, "seconds": 1.1, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "init", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 33.223517417907715, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 9.24044418334961, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.0245815, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:52:55.134165+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: How does the ValidationError.messages property maintain architectural consistency between two distinct error representation formats while avoiding redundant data traversal in the aggregation layer?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 33221, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_020_codegraph.json"}
{"task_id": "django_020", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "graphify", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "`ValidationError` supports two normalized internal shapes:\n\n- `error_dict`: field names mapped to error lists.\n- `error_list`: a flat list of errors.\n\nThe `messages` property keeps both representations consistent by using `ValidationError.__iter__()` as the single formatting path:\n\n- For dictionary-backed errors, `dict(self)` invokes `__iter__()`, which produces each field\u2019s fully rendered message list. `messages` then flattens those values with `sum(..., [])`.\n- For list-backed errors, `list(self)` invokes the same iterator directly.\n\nThis means interpolation (`message %= params`) and string conversion happen in one canonical place\u2014`__iter__()`\u2014rather than being duplicated in `messages`.\n\nIt also avoids redundant aggregation work: the dictionary branch calls `dict(self)` directly instead of routing through `message_dict`, which would merely perform another `error_dict` check and then return the same `dict(self)`. Thus, the aggregation layer builds the rendered dictionary once and only flattens its already-processed values.\n\nRelevant code: [django/core/exceptions.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/core/exceptions.py:190), particularly `message_dict`, `messages`, and `__iter__()`.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 8, "relevance": 9, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 11301, "output_tokens": 1146, "total_tokens": 12447, "cache_read_tokens": 84480, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 4, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/core/exceptions.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "graphify", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "graphify", "server_name": "graphify", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify-mcp.exe", "launch_args": ["--transport", "stdio", "--graph", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django/graphify-out/graph.json"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__graphify__query_graph", "mcp__graphify__get_node", "mcp__graphify__get_neighbors", "mcp__graphify__shortest_path", "mcp__graphify__get_community", "mcp__graphify__god_nodes", "mcp__graphify__graph_stats"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify.exe", "update", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["get_community", "get_neighbors", "get_node", "get_pr_impact", "god_nodes", "graph_stats", "list_prs", "query_graph", "shortest_path", "triage_prs"], "served_count": 10, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__graphify__query_graph", "mcp__graphify__get_node"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"graphify": {"ok": 2, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "graphify", "rc": 0, "seconds": 211.2, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify.exe", "update", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 49.47101950645447, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 8.60646677017212, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.03614625, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:53:37.999102+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: How does the ValidationError.messages property maintain architectural consistency between two distinct error representation formats while avoiding redundant data traversal in the aggregation layer?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 49468, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_020_graphify.json"}
{"task_id": "django_020", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "code-review-graph", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "`ValidationError.messages` acts as a normalization boundary over Django\u2019s two internal validation-error representations:\n\n- Field-specific errors are stored in `self.error_dict`.\n- Non-field or flat errors are stored in `self.error_list`.\n\nIn [`django/core/exceptions.py`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/core/exceptions.py:199), the property preserves consistent behavior by delegating conversion to `ValidationError.__iter__()`:\n\n```python\n@property\ndef messages(self):\n    if hasattr(self, \"error_dict\"):\n        return sum(dict(self).values(), [])\n    return list(self)\n```\n\nFor dictionary-backed errors, `dict(self)` invokes [`ValidationError.__iter__()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/core/exceptions.py:212), which converts each field\u2019s errors into finalized message strings. `messages` then discards the field keys and flattens those already-converted lists.\n\nFor list-backed errors, `list(self)` uses the same iterator to interpolate parameters and stringify every message.\n\nThis maintains architectural consistency because both formats pass through one canonical rendering path\u2014`__iter__()`\u2014instead of duplicating message formatting inside `messages`. It also avoids redundant traversal in the aggregation layer: the dictionary branch builds the rendered mapping once and flattens its values directly, while the list branch consumes the iterator once. Thus `messages` only aggregates; normalization, parameter substitution, and string conversion remain centralized in `__iter__()`.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 17446, "output_tokens": 803, "total_tokens": 18249, "cache_read_tokens": 89856, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 3, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/core/exceptions.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "code-review-graph", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "code-review-graph", "server_name": "crg", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "launch_args": ["serve", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__crg__semantic_search_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__query_graph_tool", "mcp__crg__traverse_graph_tool", "mcp__crg__get_flow_tool", "mcp__crg__list_flows_tool", "mcp__crg__get_affected_flows_tool", "mcp__crg__get_architecture_overview_tool", "mcp__crg__get_bridge_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__get_community_tool", "mcp__crg__list_communities_tool", "mcp__crg__get_hub_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__get_impact_radius_tool", "mcp__crg__get_knowledge_gaps_tool", "mcp__crg__get_minimal_context_tool", "mcp__crg__get_docs_section_tool", "mcp__crg__get_wiki_page_tool", "mcp__crg__get_suggested_questions_tool", "mcp__crg__get_surprising_connections_tool", "mcp__crg__find_large_functions_tool", "mcp__crg__list_graph_stats_tool"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "build", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django", "--embedding-provider", "local", "--embedding-model", "sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["apply_refactor_tool", "build_or_update_graph_tool", "cross_repo_search_tool", "detect_changes_tool", "embed_graph_tool", "find_large_functions_tool", "generate_wiki_tool", "get_affected_flows_tool", "get_architecture_overview_tool", "get_bridge_nodes_tool", "get_community_tool", "get_docs_section_tool", "get_flow_tool", "get_hub_nodes_tool", "get_impact_radius_tool", "get_knowledge_gaps_tool", "get_minimal_context_tool", "get_review_context_tool", "get_suggested_questions_tool", "get_surprising_connections_tool", "get_wiki_page_tool", "list_communities_tool", "list_flows_tool", "list_graph_stats_tool", "list_repos_tool", "query_graph_tool", "refactor_tool", "run_postprocess_tool", "semantic_search_nodes_tool", "traverse_graph_tool"], "served_count": 30, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__crg__get_minimal_context_tool", "mcp__crg__semantic_search_nodes_tool"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"crg": {"ok": 2, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "code-review-graph", "rc": 0, "seconds": 259.0, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "build", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django", "--embedding-provider", "local", "--embedding-model", "sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 52.096572399139404, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 9.656993865966797, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.0410695, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:54:40.501099+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: How does the ValidationError.messages property maintain architectural consistency between two distinct error representation formats while avoiding redundant data traversal in the aggregation layer?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 52094, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_020_code-review-graph.json"}
{"task_id": "django_020", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "serena", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "`ValidationError` supports two normalized internal representations:\n\n- `error_list` for a single error or a list of errors.\n- `error_dict` for field-keyed errors.\n\nNormalization happens in `ValidationError.__init__()` in [django/core/exceptions.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/core/exceptions.py:147).\n\nThe `messages` property keeps these representations architecturally consistent by routing both through the class\u2019s iterator:\n\n```python\nif hasattr(self, \"error_dict\"):\n    return sum(dict(self).values(), [])\nreturn list(self)\n```\n\n[django/core/exceptions.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/core/exceptions.py:198)\n\nThis matters because `ValidationError.__iter__()` is the single conversion boundary that performs parameter substitution and converts messages to strings. For dictionary errors, it produces `(field, rendered_messages)` pairs; for list errors, it produces rendered message strings directly ([django/core/exceptions.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/core/exceptions.py:212)).\n\nThus:\n\n- `list(self)` obtains the flat representation directly.\n- `dict(self)` obtains the field-keyed representation through exactly the same rendering logic, after which `sum(...values(), [])` only removes the field grouping.\n\nThe aggregation layer therefore does not independently walk each underlying `ValidationError`, interpolate parameters, or stringify messages again. It delegates that traversal and formatting to `__iter__()` and performs only the format-specific final operation: preserve the list or flatten the dictionary\u2019s already-rendered values. `message_dict` follows the same design by returning `dict(self)` rather than maintaining separate conversion logic ([django/core/exceptions.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/core/exceptions.py:190)).\n\nThe expected dictionary flattening behavior is explicitly covered by `TestValidationError.test_messages_concatenates_error_dict_values()` in [tests/test_exceptions/test_validation_error.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/tests/test_exceptions/test_validation_error.py:8).", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 26356, "output_tokens": 1219, "total_tokens": 27575, "cache_read_tokens": 148224, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 7, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": [], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "serena", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "serena", "server_name": "serena", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/serena.exe", "launch_args": ["start-mcp-server", "--project", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-serena-django-django", "--transport", "stdio", "--enable-web-dashboard", "false", "--enable-gui-log-window", "false"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__get_symbols_overview", "mcp__serena__find_declaration", "mcp__serena__find_implementations", "mcp__serena__find_referencing_symbols", "mcp__serena__get_diagnostics_for_file", "mcp__serena__find_file", "mcp__serena__list_dir", "mcp__serena__read_file"], "index": null, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-serena-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["activate_project", "create_text_file", "delete_memory", "edit_memory", "execute_shell_command", "find_declaration", "find_file", "find_implementations", "find_referencing_symbols", "find_symbol", "get_current_config", "get_diagnostics_for_file", "get_symbols_overview", "initial_instructions", "insert_after_symbol", "insert_before_symbol", "list_dir", "list_memories", "onboarding", "read_file", "read_memory", "rename_memory", "rename_symbol", "replace_content", "replace_in_files", "replace_symbol_body", "safe_delete_symbol", "search_for_pattern", "write_memory"], "served_count": 29, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__serena__initial_instructions", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_referencing_symbols"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"serena": {"ok": 7, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"skipped": "no-index-by-design", "seconds": 0.0}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 90.08468294143677, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 8.404732704162598, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.063663, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:56:01.639904+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: How does the ValidationError.messages property maintain architectural consistency between two distinct error representation formats while avoiding redundant data traversal in the aggregation layer?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 90082, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_020_serena.json"}
{"task_id": "django_028", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "c0-bare", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The regression comes from duplicate per-element work in PostgreSQL\u2019s `SimpleArrayField`, not merely from Django\u2019s normal three-stage validation pipeline.\n\n[`Field.clean()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/forms/fields.py:202) sequentially performs:\n\n```python\nvalue = self.to_python(value)\nself.validate(value)\nself.run_validators(value)\n```\n\n`SimpleArrayField` overrides every one of these stages:\n\n- [`to_python()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/contrib/postgres/forms/array.py:43) converts every array item.\n- [`validate()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/contrib/postgres/forms/array.py:68) validates every item.\n- [`run_validators()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/contrib/postgres/forms/array.py:86) runs every base-field validator for every item.\n\nHowever, [`SimpleArrayField.clean()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/contrib/postgres/forms/array.py:32) then cleans every item again:\n\n```python\nvalue = super().clean(value)\nreturn [self.base_field.clean(val) for val in value]\n```\n\nBecause `super().clean()` dynamically calls the overridden methods above, one call processes an array as follows:\n\n1. Traverse all items and call `base_field.to_python()`.\n2. Traverse them again and call `base_field.validate()`.\n3. Traverse them again and call `base_field.run_validators()`.\n4. Traverse them a fourth time and call `base_field.clean()`, which repeats all three operations.\n\nThus each item\u2019s conversion and validation stages run twice. For `n` items and `v` validators, the work remains asymptotically linear\u2014roughly `O(n \u00d7 v)`\u2014but with almost twice the expensive conversions and validator calls, four Python-level traversals, repeated exception machinery, and extra list allocation. Nested `SimpleArrayField` instances amplify the duplication at every nesting level.\n\nThe direct optimization is to remove the redundant `SimpleArrayField.clean()` override, or reduce it to:\n\n```python\ndef clean(self, value):\n    return super().clean(value)\n```\n\nThe superclass pipeline already returns the converted list and invokes all three item-aware overrides, so functionality and indexed validation errors are retained without calling `base_field.clean()` again. A regression test should use an instrumented base field and assert that `to_python()`, `validate()`, and `run_validators()` are each called exactly once per item.\n\nThe same pattern should be avoided in other collection fields: either implement the three phase-specific traversals and rely on `Field.clean()`, or perform a single combined per-item `clean()` traversal\u2014not both. For bulk model validation, [`Model.clean_fields()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/models/base.py:1737) also calls each model field\u2019s `clean()` per instance, so removing this duplication matters proportionally more as dataset size grows.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 8, "completeness": 7, "relevance": 7, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 25745, "output_tokens": 1904, "total_tokens": 27649, "cache_read_tokens": 98048, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 4, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/contrib/postgres/fields/array.py", "django/contrib/postgres/forms/array.py", "django/db/models/base.py", "django/db/models/fields/__init__.py", "django/forms/fields.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "c0-bare", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "c0-bare", "server_name": null, "launch_command": null, "launch_args": null, "served_tools": null, "client_tools": [], "index": null, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-c0-bare-django-django"}, "served_tools": [], "served_count": null, "mcp_tools_issued": [], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": null, "mcp_per_server": {}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 65.90963816642761, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 8.749905824661255, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.06347725, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:57:50.556651+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why is there a performance regression caused by the clean() method's sequential invocation of to_python(), validate(), and run_validators() when processing large datasets, and how can this be optimized?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 65908, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_028_c0-bare.json"}
{"task_id": "django_028", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "repowise", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The regression comes from applying a deliberately scalar validation pipeline to a large dataset.\n\nIn [`Field.clean()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:861), every value is processed as:\n\n```python\nvalue = self.to_python(value)\nself.validate(value, model_instance)\nself.run_validators(value)\n```\n\nThen [`Model.clean_fields()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/models/base.py:1737) repeats that pipeline for every field of every model instance. Consequently, validating `N` objects with `F` fields costs approximately:\n\n```text\nN \u00d7 F \u00d7 (conversion + built-in validation + all custom validators)\n```\n\nThe important costs are:\n\n- `to_python()` repeatedly parses or converts each raw value.\n- [`validate()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:830) performs null/blank checks and may linearly scan `choices`.\n- [`run_validators()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:814) creates an error list and invokes every validator for every nonempty value. It intentionally collects all validator errors rather than stopping after the first one.\n- `clean_fields()` performs Python attribute lookup, assignment, and exception handling around each field.\n\nThe same sequential design exists for forms in [`django/forms/fields.py`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/forms/fields.py:202).\n\nThe three stages cannot simply run concurrently: `validate()` and validators must receive the converted result from `to_python()`, and changing validator execution to fail-fast would change Django\u2019s error-aggregation behavior.\n\nOptimization should therefore happen at the dataset boundary:\n\n- For trusted or already validated imports, avoid calling `full_clean()` per object. Validate the incoming columns in batches and then use the bulk persistence path.\n- If Django validation remains necessary, pass `exclude` so [`clean_fields()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/models/base.py:1737) skips irrelevant fields.\n- Move expensive, pure validation into vectorized or set-based preprocessing, then retain `Field.clean()` only for values requiring Django-specific checks.\n- Cache conversion or validation results for repeated values only when the conversion and validators are pure and context-independent.\n- Remove duplicate validators and optimize individual custom validators\u2014for example, precompute lookup sets instead of repeatedly performing linear searches or database queries.\n- For a custom field, an optimized `clean()` may combine conversion and checks into one pass, but it must preserve converted-value semantics and `ValidationError` behavior.\n\nIn short, method-call sequencing itself is required for correctness; the regression arises because that scalar sequence is multiplied across every field and row. Batching, excluding unnecessary work, and optimizing expensive validators are safer than attempting to parallelize the three dependent stages.", "judge_scores": {"error": "Command '['claude', '-p', 'You are evaluating an AI agent\\'s answer to a repository-level code question.\\n\\nQUESTION:\\nWhy is there a performance regression caused by the clean() method\\'s sequential "}, "input_tokens": 32126, "output_tokens": 1289, "total_tokens": 33415, "cache_read_tokens": 78592, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 2, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/db/models/base.py", "django/db/models/fields/__init__.py", "django/forms/fields.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer"], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "repowise", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "repowise", "server_name": "repowise", "launch_command": "C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/repowise-layerb/.venv/Scripts/repowise.exe", "launch_args": ["mcp", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-repowise-full-django-django", "--transport", "stdio"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer", "mcp__repowise__get_context", "mcp__repowise__get_overview", "mcp__repowise__get_risk", "mcp__repowise__get_symbol", "mcp__repowise__get_why", "mcp__repowise__search_codebase"], "index": null, "shares_index_with": "repowise-full", "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-repowise-full-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["get_answer", "get_change_risk", "get_context", "get_dead_code", "get_health", "get_overview", "get_risk", "get_symbol", "get_why", "list_repos", "search_codebase"], "served_count": 11, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"repowise": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"builtin": "repowise_legacy", "rc": 0, "seconds": 0.0, "index_vector_dim": 1536, "index_embedder_mock": false}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 57.221189737319946, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 90.05060076713562, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.0628715, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:59:05.346895+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why is there a performance regression caused by the clean() method's sequential invocation of to_python(), validate(), and run_validators() when processing large datasets, and how can this be optimized?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 57219, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_028_repowise.json"}
{"task_id": "django_028", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "codegraph", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The regression comes from multiplicative per-value work, not merely from having three method calls.\n\nIn both model and form fields, `clean()` is deliberately a sequential validation pipeline:\n\n- [`django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:861`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:861)\n- [`django/forms/fields.py:202`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/forms/fields.py:202)\n\nConceptually:\n\n```python\nvalue = self.to_python(value)\nself.validate(value, model_instance)  # forms omit model_instance\nself.run_validators(value)\nreturn value\n```\n\nThe ordering is required:\n\n1. `to_python()` converts raw input to the type expected by validation.\n2. `validate()` checks field semantics such as `required`, `blank`, `null`, and `choices`.\n3. `run_validators()` invokes every configured validator on the converted, structurally valid value.\n\nFor `N` objects with `F` fields and `V` validators, this is approximately `O(N \u00d7 F \u00d7 V)`, plus conversion and built-in validation. The cost becomes significant because:\n\n- [`Model.clean_fields()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/base.py:1737) loops through every model field and calls `f.clean()` for each object.\n- [`Field.run_validators()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:814) loops through all validators and constructs/aggregates `ValidationError` objects.\n- Model-field choice validation at [`django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:830`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:830) scans choices sequentially, making large choice collections especially expensive.\n- Composite fields multiply the work. For example, `SimpleArrayField` runs conversion, validation, and validators over every array element in [`django/contrib/postgres/forms/array.py:43`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/contrib/postgres/forms/array.py:43), [`:68`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/contrib/postgres/forms/array.py:68), and [`:86`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/contrib/postgres/forms/array.py:86).\n- Database-backed validators, uniqueness checks, or constraints can introduce an N+1-query problem. [`Model.full_clean()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/base.py:1690) additionally calls `validate_unique()` and `validate_constraints()`.\n\nHow to optimize it:\n\n- Do not call `full_clean()` independently for every object when importing already trusted or prevalidated data. Normalize and validate the input in batches, then use the ORM\u2019s bulk insertion path.\n- If validation is required, batch expensive checks: collect keys first, fetch existing database values in one query, validate membership using sets, and then construct objects.\n- Call only the necessary validation layers. `full_clean(validate_unique=False, validate_constraints=False)` avoids database-oriented checks when they will be enforced separately or by database constraints.\n- Pass `exclude` to `full_clean()`/`clean_fields()` when only a subset of fields needs validation. `clean_fields()` also already skips excluded/generated fields, blank empty values, and `DatabaseDefault` values.\n- Replace large, repeatedly scanned `choices` collections with precomputed set/dictionary membership in a custom field or validator when appropriate.\n- Consolidate multiple validators into one pass when they repeat expensive parsing or queries. Cache immutable derived state outside the validator call.\n- For array or other composite fields, parse each item once and perform combined validation in the same traversal rather than repeating equivalent work across overridden phases.\n\nThe three phases generally should not be parallelized or reordered because later phases depend on the converted result and Django\u2019s validation/error semantics. The effective optimization is to reduce how often the pipeline runs, reduce the work inside each phase, and batch any database-dependent validation.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 8, "completeness": 8, "reasoning": 8, "clarity": 8, "relevance": 8}, "input_tokens": 14012, "output_tokens": 1472, "total_tokens": 15484, "cache_read_tokens": 61440, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 2, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": [], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "codegraph", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "codegraph", "server_name": "codegraph", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "launch_args": ["serve", "--mcp", "--path", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django", "--no-watch"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "init", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["codegraph_explore"], "served_count": 1, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore", "mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"codegraph": {"ok": 2, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "codegraph", "rc": 0, "seconds": 1.1, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "init", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 49.674846172332764, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 17.20138907432556, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.039915, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:01:36.820621+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why is there a performance regression caused by the clean() method's sequential invocation of to_python(), validate(), and run_validators() when processing large datasets, and how can this be optimized?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 49672, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_028_codegraph.json"}
{"task_id": "django_028", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "graphify", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The regression comes from multiplying a deliberately staged, per-value validation pipeline across every field and every record.\n\nIn both form and model fields, `clean()` performs three ordered operations:\n\n- `to_python()` converts or parses the raw value.\n- `validate()` performs built-in checks such as required/null/blank and choices.\n- `run_validators()` invokes every configured validator and aggregates their errors.\n\nSee [django/forms/fields.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/forms/fields.py:202) and [django/db/models/fields/__init__.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:861).\n\nFor `N` objects with `F` fields and an average of `V` validators, validation is approximately `O(N \u00d7 F \u00d7 V)`, plus conversion and built-in validation. The constant costs can also be substantial:\n\n- Conversions may parse dates, decimals, URLs, UUIDs, or other structured values.\n- Model `validate()` scans choices linearly, including optgroups.\n- `run_validators()` deliberately executes all validators so it can collect every error rather than stopping at the first failure.\n- Custom validators may perform expensive computation or database queries.\n- Composite fields amplify the work: `ComboField.clean()` invokes multiple child-field pipelines, while `MultiValueField.clean()` cleans every constituent field in [django/forms/fields.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/forms/fields.py:1042) and [django/forms/fields.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/forms/fields.py:1098).\n- `Model.clean_fields()` repeats this for every model field and assigns each converted result back to the instance in [django/db/models/base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/models/base.py:1737).\n\nThe three phases cannot generally be reordered or independently parallelized: validation and validators must receive the converted value, and subclass overrides depend on these public hooks and their exception behavior.\n\nThe effective optimization is therefore at the dataset boundary:\n\n- Validate only untrusted or changed fields, using `full_clean(exclude=...)` or `clean_fields(exclude=...)`.\n- Skip application-level cleaning for already typed, trusted data. Notably, Django\u2019s `bulk_create()` does not call `full_clean()`, so validated objects can be inserted in batches.\n- Move dataset-wide checks out of per-row validators. Prefetch reference data once, validate membership in memory, and replace per-value database validators with set-based queries.\n- Cache immutable lookup structures\u2014for example, flatten large choices into a set instead of repeatedly scanning them.\n- Pre-convert homogeneous columns in batches and reuse the converted values, avoiding repeated parsing.\n- For custom fields, fuse cheap conversion and validation into a specialized `clean()` only when profiling proves dispatch overhead matters, while preserving conversion-before-validation and Django-compatible `ValidationError` behavior.\n- If complete error aggregation is unnecessary, a custom ingestion path can fail fast instead of running every validator.\n\nThe main gain will usually come from eliminating repeated I/O and redundant validation, not merely combining the three method calls; their Python dispatch overhead is generally smaller than parsing, choice scans, and custom validators.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 8, "relevance": 9, "clarity": 8, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 29387, "output_tokens": 1861, "total_tokens": 31248, "cache_read_tokens": 208640, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 7, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/db/models/base.py", "django/db/models/fields/__init__.py", "django/db/models/query.py", "django/forms/fields.py", "django/forms/forms.py", "django/forms/models.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "graphify", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "graphify", "server_name": "graphify", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify-mcp.exe", "launch_args": ["--transport", "stdio", "--graph", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django/graphify-out/graph.json"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__graphify__query_graph", "mcp__graphify__get_node", "mcp__graphify__get_neighbors", "mcp__graphify__shortest_path", "mcp__graphify__get_community", "mcp__graphify__god_nodes", "mcp__graphify__graph_stats"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify.exe", "update", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["get_community", "get_neighbors", "get_node", "get_pr_impact", "god_nodes", "graph_stats", "list_prs", "query_graph", "shortest_path", "triage_prs"], "served_count": 10, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__graphify__query_graph", "mcp__graphify__get_node"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"graphify": {"ok": 2, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "graphify", "rc": 0, "seconds": 211.2, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify.exe", "update", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 78.77131009101868, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 8.632344722747803, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.08142375, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:02:44.185574+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why is there a performance regression caused by the clean() method's sequential invocation of to_python(), validate(), and run_validators() when processing large datasets, and how can this be optimized?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 78768, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_028_graphify.json"}
{"task_id": "django_028", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "code-review-graph", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The regression comes from multiplying a deliberately sequential, per-value validation pipeline across every field and every record.\n\nIn both model and form fields, `clean()` performs three separate stages:\n\n```python\nvalue = self.to_python(value)\nself.validate(value, model_instance)\nself.run_validators(value)\n```\n\nSee [django/db/models/fields/\\_\\_init\\_\\_.py:861](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:861) and the equivalent form implementation in [django/forms/fields.py:202](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/forms/fields.py:202).\n\nWhy it becomes expensive:\n\n- `Model.clean_fields()` loops over every model field and invokes `f.clean()` individually ([django/db/models/base.py:1737](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/base.py:1737)).\n- Form validation similarly cleans every bound field ([django/forms/forms.py:341](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/forms/forms.py:341)).\n- Each invocation incurs conversion, built-in validation, and then another loop over all configured validators. `run_validators()` also catches and aggregates every validation error ([django/db/models/fields/\\_\\_init\\_\\_.py:814](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:814)).\n- Choice validation can additionally scan choices linearly.\n- Consequently, processing `N` objects with `F` fields and roughly `V` validators costs approximately `O(N \u00d7 F \u00d7 V)`, plus conversion and choice-checking costs. Python method dispatch, exception handling, allocation of error lists, and repeated parsing become visible at large `N`.\n\nThe three stages cannot generally be parallelized or reordered: `validate()` and validators must receive the value produced by `to_python()`, and a conversion or built-in-validation failure must stop later stages. Parallel execution would also alter validator ordering and error semantics.\n\nPractical optimization:\n\n- For trusted bulk imports, normalize and validate data once in a batch-oriented ingestion layer, then use `QuerySet.bulk_create()` in suitable batches. Its implementation avoids per-object `save()` and signals ([django/db/models/query.py:792](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/query.py:792)). Note that `bulk_create()` does not supply application-level validation, so database constraints should protect invariants.\n- When `full_clean()` is required, pass `exclude` to avoid fields already validated or irrelevant to the operation. `clean_fields()` explicitly skips excluded, generated, blank-empty, and database-default fields.\n- Make custom `to_python()` implementations idempotent and add a fast path when the value is already the correct Python type.\n- Consolidate multiple cheap custom validators into one validator when preserving identical error behavior is acceptable, and avoid database queries inside per-value validators. Batch-fetch reference data into sets/maps instead.\n- For large choice collections, use set/dictionary membership or a relational lookup rather than repeated linear scans.\n- Cache immutable validation metadata. Django already caches the assembled model-field `validators` list with `cached_property`, so optimization should focus on validator work rather than reconstructing that list.\n\nA framework-level fused `clean()` might shave method-call overhead, but it would complicate subclass overrides and provide limited benefit compared with eliminating repeated validation, batching external lookups, and reducing the number of fields and records entering this per-value pipeline.", "judge_scores": {"error": "Command '['claude', '-p', 'You are evaluating an AI agent\\'s answer to a repository-level code question.\\n\\nQUESTION:\\nWhy is there a performance regression caused by the clean() method\\'s sequential "}, "input_tokens": 23999, "output_tokens": 1841, "total_tokens": 25840, "cache_read_tokens": 100608, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 9, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/db/models/base.py", "django/db/models/fields/__init__.py", "django/db/models/query.py", "django/forms/fields.py", "django/forms/forms.py", "django\\\\db\\\\models\\\\base.py", "django\\\\db\\\\models\\\\fields\\\\__init__.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "code-review-graph", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "code-review-graph", "server_name": "crg", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "launch_args": ["serve", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__crg__semantic_search_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__query_graph_tool", "mcp__crg__traverse_graph_tool", "mcp__crg__get_flow_tool", "mcp__crg__list_flows_tool", "mcp__crg__get_affected_flows_tool", "mcp__crg__get_architecture_overview_tool", "mcp__crg__get_bridge_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__get_community_tool", "mcp__crg__list_communities_tool", "mcp__crg__get_hub_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__get_impact_radius_tool", "mcp__crg__get_knowledge_gaps_tool", "mcp__crg__get_minimal_context_tool", "mcp__crg__get_docs_section_tool", "mcp__crg__get_wiki_page_tool", "mcp__crg__get_suggested_questions_tool", "mcp__crg__get_surprising_connections_tool", "mcp__crg__find_large_functions_tool", "mcp__crg__list_graph_stats_tool"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "build", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django", "--embedding-provider", "local", "--embedding-model", "sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["apply_refactor_tool", "build_or_update_graph_tool", "cross_repo_search_tool", "detect_changes_tool", "embed_graph_tool", "find_large_functions_tool", "generate_wiki_tool", "get_affected_flows_tool", "get_architecture_overview_tool", "get_bridge_nodes_tool", "get_community_tool", "get_docs_section_tool", "get_flow_tool", "get_hub_nodes_tool", "get_impact_radius_tool", "get_knowledge_gaps_tool", "get_minimal_context_tool", "get_review_context_tool", "get_suggested_questions_tool", "get_surprising_connections_tool", "get_wiki_page_tool", "list_communities_tool", "list_flows_tool", "list_graph_stats_tool", "list_repos_tool", "query_graph_tool", "refactor_tool", "run_postprocess_tool", "semantic_search_nodes_tool", "traverse_graph_tool"], "served_count": 30, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__crg__semantic_search_nodes_tool"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"crg": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "code-review-graph", "rc": 0, "seconds": 259.0, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "build", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django", "--embedding-provider", "local", "--embedding-model", "sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 82.51201176643372, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 90.04550361633301, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.060984750000000004, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:04:16.169404+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why is there a performance regression caused by the clean() method's sequential invocation of to_python(), validate(), and run_validators() when processing large datasets, and how can this be optimized?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 82509, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_028_code-review-graph.json"}
{"task_id": "django_028", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "serena", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The regression comes from multiplying a deliberately scalar validation pipeline across every value in a large dataset.\n\nIn both model and form fields, `Field.clean()` performs three dependent Python calls:\n\n```python\nvalue = self.to_python(value)\nself.validate(value, model_instance)\nself.run_validators(value)\n```\n\nSee [model Field.clean()](/C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:861) and [form Field.clean()](/C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/forms/fields.py:202).\n\nWhy it becomes expensive:\n\n- The stages cannot simply run concurrently: `validate()` and every validator must receive the value returned by `to_python()`.\n- `Model.clean_fields()` loops over every model field and invokes `f.clean()` independently ([base.py](/C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/base.py:1757)). For `N` objects, `F` fields, and `V` validators, the work is roughly `O(N \u00d7 F \u00d7 V)`.\n- Every value incurs multiple Python method dispatches, repeated empty-value membership checks, exception-handling setup, and an allocation of `errors = []`.\n- Model `Field.validate()` scans all `choices`, including nested option groups, linearly for every value ([fields/__init__.py](/C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:830)).\n- `run_validators()` invokes every validator separately and deliberately collects all failures instead of stopping after the first ([fields/__init__.py](/C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:814)).\n- Container fields amplify this: PostgreSQL `SimpleArrayField.clean()` calls `base_field.clean()` once per element ([array.py](/C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/contrib/postgres/forms/array.py:31)).\n\nThe main optimization should therefore happen at the dataset boundary, not by reordering these three correctness-dependent stages:\n\n1. Convert and validate in batches before constructing or saving models. Cache field objects and validator callables outside the row loop.\n2. Replace per-row validators that perform I/O\u2014especially database lookups\u2014with one bulk query, then validate using sets or dictionaries in memory.\n3. Precompute immutable lookup structures such as a set of allowed choice keys instead of repeatedly scanning `choices`.\n4. Use `bulk_create()` after batch validation rather than calling `full_clean()` for every instance. Keep database constraints as the final integrity boundary.\n5. If all validation errors are not required, use a specialized fail-fast bulk path; the repository\u2019s `run_validators()` intentionally accumulates every error, so changing it globally would alter Django\u2019s error semantics.\n6. Avoid rebuilding validator lists\u2014the model field already helps here by defining `validators` as a `cached_property` at [fields/__init__.py](/C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:806).\n\nInlining the three calls might shave off minor dispatch overhead, but it does not change the dominant scaling behavior. Batch conversion, set-based checks, bulk I/O, and avoiding unnecessary per-instance `full_clean()` calls provide the meaningful improvement while preserving the required conversion-before-validation order.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 8, "completeness": 7, "relevance": 8, "clarity": 8, "reasoning": 8}, "input_tokens": 30367, "output_tokens": 1464, "total_tokens": 31831, "cache_read_tokens": 132096, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 10, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": [], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "serena", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "serena", "server_name": "serena", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/serena.exe", "launch_args": ["start-mcp-server", "--project", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-serena-django-django", "--transport", "stdio", "--enable-web-dashboard", "false", "--enable-gui-log-window", "false"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__get_symbols_overview", "mcp__serena__find_declaration", "mcp__serena__find_implementations", "mcp__serena__find_referencing_symbols", "mcp__serena__get_diagnostics_for_file", "mcp__serena__find_file", "mcp__serena__list_dir", "mcp__serena__read_file"], "index": null, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-serena-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["activate_project", "create_text_file", "delete_memory", "edit_memory", "execute_shell_command", "find_declaration", "find_file", "find_implementations", "find_referencing_symbols", "find_symbol", "get_current_config", "get_diagnostics_for_file", "get_symbols_overview", "initial_instructions", "insert_after_symbol", "insert_before_symbol", "list_dir", "list_memories", "onboarding", "read_file", "read_memory", "rename_memory", "rename_symbol", "replace_content", "replace_in_files", "replace_symbol_body", "safe_delete_symbol", "search_for_pattern", "write_memory"], "served_count": 29, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__serena__initial_instructions", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__search_for_pattern"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"serena": {"ok": 10, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"skipped": "no-index-by-design", "seconds": 0.0}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 61.2012574672699, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 7.192196607589722, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.06911075, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:07:26.472865+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why is there a performance regression caused by the clean() method's sequential invocation of to_python(), validate(), and run_validators() when processing large datasets, and how can this be optimized?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 61199, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_028_serena.json"}
{"task_id": "django_031", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "c0-bare", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The impact is cumulative because ORM field metadata sits directly inside very hot Python loops. A single property lookup is cheap, but ORM operations repeatedly inspect every field for every model instance, query column, insert, update, and validation pass. Millions of accesses turn descriptor dispatch, function calls, condition checks, and occasional allocations into measurable CPU time.\n\nConcrete examples in this repository:\n\n- `Field.choices` is a property whose getter returns `_choices`; every read invokes Python\u2019s property descriptor. Assignment also normalizes the value in the setter ([fields/__init__.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:575)).\n- `db_tablespace` performs a fallback through Django\u2019s lazy settings object on every access ([fields/__init__.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:960)).\n- `db_returning` calls `has_db_default()`, adding both descriptor and method-call overhead each time ([fields/__init__.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:964)).\n- `flatchoices` rebuilds a list from `flatten_choices(self.choices)` on every access, so its cost includes iteration and allocation, not just property dispatch ([fields/__init__.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:1124)).\n- Lazy values such as `cached_col`, `validators`, `error_messages`, `unique`, `_get_default`, and `_db_default_expression` use `cached_property`. Their first access invokes `cached_property.__get__()`, computes the result, and writes it into the field\u2019s `__dict__` ([functional.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/utils/functional.py:7)). Later accesses are faster because the instance value bypasses this non-data descriptor, but every distinct field still pays the initialization cost.\n- `cached_col`, for example, lazily imports and constructs a `Col`; it is reached by `Field.get_col()` during query compilation ([fields/__init__.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:566)).\n- `set_attributes_from_name()` already materializes stable metadata such as `attname`, `column`, and `concrete` when the field is attached to its model ([fields/__init__.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:969)). These direct attributes are much better suited to hot ORM paths.\n\nThis matters because the ORM repeatedly does things such as `field.attname`, `field.column`, `field.generated`, and `field.db_returning`. For example, model construction loops over concrete fields and reads `field.attname` in [base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/models/base.py:520), while bulk creation repeatedly filters on `generated`, reads `attname`, and processes returning fields in [query.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/models/query.py:849). A few extra Python operations multiplied by fields \u00d7 objects \u00d7 ORM phases produce the cumulative cost.\n\nThe main optimizations are:\n\n- Materialize immutable or model-bound values once. During `__init__()` or `contribute_to_class()`, store frequently accessed booleans and strings as ordinary attributes, as is already done for `is_relation`, `attname`, `column`, and `concrete`.\n- Replace trivial read-only properties with direct attributes where compatibility permits. For example, a precomputed `db_returning` boolean avoids a property call plus `has_db_default()` on every read.\n- Cache allocation-heavy derived values. `flatchoices` is a strong candidate for `cached_property`; the `choices` setter must then invalidate `self.__dict__[\"flatchoices\"]` whenever choices change.\n- Resolve `db_tablespace` once if runtime changes through `override_settings()` do not need to remain observable. Otherwise, preserve its dynamic property semantics.\n- Keep genuinely optional expensive objects lazy. Eagerly constructing every `cached_col`, validator list, or default expression could waste memory and startup time for fields never used in that way. Another option is to \u201cwarm\u201d only the metadata known to be needed when model options are finalized.\n- In ORM loops, bind repeated values once\u2014such as `attname = field.attname` or precompute tuples of field metadata in `Options`\u2014instead of repeatedly traversing descriptors.\n- Add explicit invalidation whenever mutable inputs change. Caching `choices`, `db_default`, `_unique`, or `primary_key`-derived results without invalidation would create stale metadata.\n\nIn short: laziness is useful for expensive, rarely needed field state, but trivial accessors and repeatedly reconstructed values are poor fits for the ORM\u2019s hottest loops. The best balance is direct attributes for stable metadata, cached computation for expensive derived metadata, selective laziness for optional objects, and carefully defined cache invalidation for mutable field configuration.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 9, "clarity": 8, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 30100, "output_tokens": 2399, "total_tokens": 32499, "cache_read_tokens": 146944, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 8, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/db/models/base.py", "django/db/models/fields/__init__.py", "django/db/models/fields/related.py", "django/db/models/fields/related_descriptors.py", "django/db/models/options.py", "django/db/models/query.py", "django/utils/functional.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "c0-bare", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "c0-bare", "server_name": null, "launch_command": null, "launch_args": null, "served_tools": null, "client_tools": [], "index": null, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-c0-bare-django-django"}, "served_tools": [], "served_count": null, "mcp_tools_issued": [], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": null, "mcp_per_server": {}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 76.6021192073822, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 8.817927598953247, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.079983, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:08:46.281169+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why is there a cumulative performance impact of the Field class's multiple property accessors and lazy initialization patterns when accessing field attributes millions of times in ORM operations, and how can this be optimized?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 76601, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_031_c0-bare.json"}
{"task_id": "django_031", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "repowise", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The performance impact is cumulative because ORM hot paths turn tiny per-access costs into millions of repeated Python operations.\n\nIn [`django/utils/functional.py`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/utils/functional.py:7), `cached_property` implements lazy initialization through `cached_property.__get__()`. The first access to each cached attribute:\n\n1. Invokes the descriptor.\n2. Calls the underlying function.\n3. Mutates `instance.__dict__` to store the result.\n4. Returns the computed value.\n\nSubsequent access is cheap because the instance dictionary shadows the non-data descriptor, but the initialization cost still occurs once for every property on every distinct `Field` instance. Across many fields, queries, model constructions, and millions of attribute reads, even ordinary property dispatch, function calls, condition checks, and dictionary operations become measurable. Laziness saves work only when an attribute is never used; it does not make the first access free.\n\nThe same effect becomes much larger for file fields. In [`django/db/models/fields/files.py`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/models/fields/files.py:19), `FieldFile` exposes lazy properties such as `file`, `path`, `url`, and `size`:\n\n- `_get_file()` checks `_file` and may call `storage.open()`.\n- `path` and `url` validate the file and invoke the storage backend.\n- `size` may call `storage.size()` for committed files.\n\nThus, code that appears to perform a simple attribute read inside an ORM loop may actually execute descriptor logic, branches, object initialization, or storage I/O. Repeating that per field and per row changes a nanosecond- or microsecond-scale overhead into seconds; storage calls can make it dramatically worse.\n\nPractical optimizations are:\n\n- Hoist repeated field-property reads out of inner loops and keep them in local variables.\n- Compute each needed derived value once per field or model instance.\n- Use `cached_property` only for genuinely expensive, repeatedly reused values; initialize universally needed values eagerly during field setup.\n- Where safe in internal hot paths, read stable precomputed attributes directly instead of passing repeatedly through wrapper properties.\n- Cache field-level metadata when the model class is prepared, since `Field` objects are generally long-lived schema metadata.\n- Use `.values()` or appropriately scoped `.only()`/`.defer()` queries when only primitive database values are needed.\n- Avoid reading `FieldFile.file`, `size`, `path`, or `url` while iterating large querysets. Persist frequently needed file metadata such as size in database columns or retrieve it in batches.\n- Benchmark before replacing descriptors: removing a property may save dispatch overhead but can weaken encapsulation or break lazy invalidation semantics.\n\nThe best general strategy is to move invariant metadata computation from the per-row/per-access path to field or model initialization, leaving hot ORM loops with plain instance-dictionary or local-variable reads.", "judge_scores": {"error": "Command '['claude', '-p', 'You are evaluating an AI agent\\'s answer to a repository-level code question.\\n\\nQUESTION:\\nWhy is there a cumulative performance impact of the Field class\\'s multiple prope"}, "input_tokens": 13503, "output_tokens": 891, "total_tokens": 14394, "cache_read_tokens": 43264, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 1, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": [], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer"], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "repowise", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "repowise", "server_name": "repowise", "launch_command": "C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/repowise-layerb/.venv/Scripts/repowise.exe", "launch_args": ["mcp", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-repowise-full-django-django", "--transport", "stdio"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer", "mcp__repowise__get_context", "mcp__repowise__get_overview", "mcp__repowise__get_risk", "mcp__repowise__get_symbol", "mcp__repowise__get_why", "mcp__repowise__search_codebase"], "index": null, "shares_index_with": "repowise-full", "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-repowise-full-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["get_answer", "get_change_risk", "get_context", "get_dead_code", "get_health", "get_overview", "get_risk", "get_symbol", "get_why", "list_repos", "search_codebase"], "served_count": 11, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"repowise": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"builtin": "repowise_legacy", "rc": 0, "seconds": 0.0, "index_vector_dim": 1536, "index_embedder_mock": false}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 39.21748948097229, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 90.04625868797302, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.031196750000000002, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:10:11.815488+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why is there a cumulative performance impact of the Field class's multiple property accessors and lazy initialization patterns when accessing field attributes millions of times in ORM operations, and how can this be optimized?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 39214, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_031_repowise.json"}
{"task_id": "django_031", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "codegraph", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The impact is cumulative because ORM operations repeatedly traverse the same small set of long-lived `Field` objects. Even tiny Python-level accessor costs become significant when multiplied by every field, row, query compilation, insert, update, validation, and model construction.\n\nIn [`django/db/models/fields/__init__.py`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:211), `Field.__init__()` already stores many frequently used facts directly\u2014`name`, `is_relation`, `default`, and so on. Binding a field later similarly materializes `attname`, `column`, and `concrete` in `set_attributes_from_name()` at line 969. These are cheap direct dictionary lookups.\n\nOther attributes add indirection:\n\n- `db_tablespace` and `db_returning` are ordinary `@property` accessors (lines 960 and 964). Every access performs descriptor resolution and calls Python code. `db_returning` additionally calls `has_db_default()`.\n- `flatchoices` (line 1124) is worse: every access calls `flatten_choices()` and allocates a new list. `Model._get_FIELD_display()` accesses it in [`django/db/models/base.py`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/base.py:1336).\n- `unique`, `validators`, `error_messages`, `_get_default`, `_db_default_expression`, and `cached_col` use `cached_property`. Their first access performs descriptor dispatch, calculation, and an instance-dictionary write.\n- Django\u2019s implementation in [`django/utils/functional.py`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/utils/functional.py:39) explicitly computes the value and stores it in `instance.__dict__`. Later reads are much cheaper direct instance lookups, but initialization is scattered across whichever ORM operation happens to touch each property first.\n- Some initializers are nontrivial: `error_messages` walks the class MRO, `validators` constructs lists, `_get_default` creates closures or expression objects, and `cached_col` constructs a `Col`.\n\nThe first-access costs alone usually aren\u2019t important. The larger problem is the mixture of always-executed properties, repeated allocations, cache misses across many different field instances, and dense access inside loops. For example, `Model._save_table()` repeatedly uses `f.name`, `f.attname`, `f.generated`, `field.db_returning`, and related attributes in [`django/db/models/base.py`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/base.py:1074). `Options` also repeatedly filters fields using `f.concrete`, `f.is_relation`, and `f.many_to_many` in [`django/db/models/options.py`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/options.py:581). Millions of such accesses make descriptor calls, function frames, branches, allocations, and cache population visible in aggregate.\n\nThe appropriate optimization is to materialize stable metadata once:\n\n- Store simple immutable results such as the base `Field` value of `db_returning` as instance attributes during initialization or model contribution, where subclass compatibility permits.\n- Convert repeatedly recomputed derived data such as `flatchoices` to a cache, with explicit invalidation in the `choices` setter.\n- Keep `cached_property` for genuinely expensive, rarely used values such as `cached_col` and backend-dependent validators.\n- Warm required cached values when fields are finalized if profiling shows first-use latency matters and most fields use them.\n- Cache field collections at the model level. This repository already does that for `Options.local_concrete_fields` and `Options.db_returning_fields` with `cached_property` at lines 587 and 1041.\n- In especially hot loops, bind repeatedly used attributes to local variables or precompute tuples containing the needed field metadata.\n\nCare is required: `db_tablespace` depends on settings, integer validators depend on the active database connection, subclasses override properties such as `validators`, and `choices` is mutable through its setter. Eagerly freezing those values without an invalidation strategy would improve benchmarks while introducing stale or backend-incorrect behavior. The best target is therefore profiling-proven, stable metadata and repeated allocation\u2014not indiscriminately replacing every lazy property.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 8, "reasoning": 9, "clarity": 8, "relevance": 9}, "input_tokens": 52353, "output_tokens": 2421, "total_tokens": 54774, "cache_read_tokens": 227072, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 8, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/db/models/base.py", "django/db/models/fields/__init__.py", "django/db/models/options.py", "django/db/models/sql/compiler.py", "django/utils/functional.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "codegraph", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "codegraph", "server_name": "codegraph", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "launch_args": ["serve", "--mcp", "--path", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django", "--no-watch"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "init", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["codegraph_explore"], "served_count": 1, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"codegraph": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "codegraph", "rc": 0, "seconds": 1.1, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "init", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 97.74759483337402, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 7.861792325973511, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.11803525, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:12:25.337491+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why is there a cumulative performance impact of the Field class's multiple property accessors and lazy initialization patterns when accessing field attributes millions of times in ORM operations, and how can this be optimized?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 97746, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_031_codegraph.json"}
{"task_id": "django_031", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "graphify", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The slowdown is cumulative because ORM operations repeatedly access metadata on long-lived `Field` objects. Even tiny per-access costs become significant when multiplied across every selected column, row, join, save, and validation operation.\n\nSeveral access patterns contribute:\n\n- Ordinary properties always invoke Python\u2019s descriptor machinery and a function call. Examples include `Field.choices`, `db_tablespace`, `db_returning`, and `flatchoices` in [django/db/models/fields/__init__.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:575).\n- Some properties perform additional work every time. `flatchoices` recreates a list by calling `flatten_choices(self.choices)` on every access at line 1124. `db_returning` calls `has_db_default()` at line 964.\n- Property chains compound the cost. For example, foreign-key operations repeatedly traverse `target_field`, `foreign_related_fields`, `related_fields`, and `related_model` in [related.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/models/fields/related.py:119).\n- Lazy initialization shifts computation into runtime hot paths. On the first access, Django\u2019s `cached_property.__get__()` calls the initializer and writes the result into `instance.__dict__` in [functional.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/utils/functional.py:39). Later access is cheap, but initializing many different cached attributes across many fields still creates bursts of work during query construction.\n- Cache invalidation adds complexity. Related-field path caches are explicitly removed when fields are copied in `RelatedField.__copy__()` at [related.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/models/fields/related.py:596), so they may need rebuilding.\n- These accesses occur in central loops. For example, `unique` is queried by query and SQL compiler logic, `db_returning` during model saves, and `flatchoices` during display-value lookup in [base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/models/base.py:1337).\n\nThe repository already demonstrates the preferred optimization for stable values. `set_attributes_from_name()` computes `attname`, `column`, and `concrete` once during model construction rather than exposing them as calculated properties at [fields/__init__.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:969). Likewise, `cached_col` is a `cached_property`, allowing `get_col()` to reuse one `Col` object for the model\u2019s base table at line 566.\n\nThe practical optimizations are:\n\n1. Eagerly calculate immutable field metadata during `__init__()`, `set_attributes_from_name()`, or `contribute_to_class()`, and store it as plain instance attributes. This gives ordinary `__dict__` lookup in hot loops.\n2. Use `cached_property` for expensive values that cannot be computed until models are registered, such as `related_model`, relationship paths, and resolved related fields.\n3. Cache repeatedly derived collections such as `flatchoices` when choices are static. If `choices` remains assignable, its setter must invalidate the cached flattened value:\n   ```python\n   @choices.setter\n   def choices(self, value):\n       self._choices = normalize_choices(value)\n       self.__dict__.pop(\"flatchoices\", None)\n\n   @cached_property\n   def flatchoices(self):\n       return list(flatten_choices(self.choices))\n   ```\n4. In tight ORM loops, bind repeatedly used metadata once:\n   ```python\n   unique = field.unique\n   target_field = field.target_field\n   ```\n   rather than traversing the same descriptor/property chain for every row or expression.\n5. Avoid caching values that are intentionally dynamic, such as settings-dependent `db_tablespace`, unless Django can define a reliable invalidation boundary.\n\nThe key tradeoff is memory and invalidation correctness versus CPU. Since each model normally owns only a modest, stable set of `Field` instances while those instances are read millions of times, storing a few additional precomputed values is generally a favorable trade.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 8, "completeness": 8, "reasoning": 8, "relevance": 9, "clarity": 8}, "input_tokens": 50197, "output_tokens": 2140, "total_tokens": 52337, "cache_read_tokens": 114944, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 8, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/db/models/fields/__init__.py", "django/db/models/fields/composite.py", "django/db/models/fields/related.py", "django/utils/functional.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "graphify", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "graphify", "server_name": "graphify", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify-mcp.exe", "launch_args": ["--transport", "stdio", "--graph", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django/graphify-out/graph.json"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__graphify__query_graph", "mcp__graphify__get_node", "mcp__graphify__get_neighbors", "mcp__graphify__shortest_path", "mcp__graphify__get_community", "mcp__graphify__god_nodes", "mcp__graphify__graph_stats"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify.exe", "update", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["get_community", "get_neighbors", "get_node", "get_pr_impact", "god_nodes", "graph_stats", "list_prs", "query_graph", "shortest_path", "triage_prs"], "served_count": 10, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__graphify__query_graph"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"graphify": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "graphify", "rc": 0, "seconds": 211.2, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify.exe", "update", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 81.90020227432251, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 8.126278400421143, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.09851425000000001, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:14:11.312851+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why is there a cumulative performance impact of the Field class's multiple property accessors and lazy initialization patterns when accessing field attributes millions of times in ORM operations, and how can this be optimized?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 81898, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_031_graphify.json"}
{"task_id": "django_031", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "code-review-graph", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The impact is cumulative because ORM compilation and model operations repeatedly access the same `Field` metadata, and several attributes are implemented through Python descriptors rather than plain instance attributes.\n\nKey examples in [django/db/models/fields/__init__.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:211):\n\n- `choices` is a property that calls its getter on every access; assignment also runs `normalize_choices()` ([line 575](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:575)).\n- `db_tablespace` performs a property call and fallback to `settings.DEFAULT_INDEX_TABLESPACE` every time ([line 960](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:960)).\n- `db_returning` invokes `has_db_default()` on every access ([line 964](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:964)).\n- `flatchoices` calls `flatten_choices()` and creates a new list on every access ([line 1124](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:1124)).\n- `unique`, `cached_col`, validators, `_get_default`, and `_db_default_expression` are lazily computed with `cached_property`.\n\nA normal `property` always incurs descriptor lookup plus a Python function call and whatever work the getter performs. That cost is tiny individually, but field metadata is consulted inside loops during query construction, inserts, updates, relation traversal, validation, and SQL compilation. For example, `unique` is repeatedly tested in [compiler.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/sql/compiler.py:1196), [query.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/query.py:1223), and [related.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/fields/related.py:679). Millions of descriptor calls, branches, method calls, and temporary allocations therefore become measurable CPU time.\n\nThe lazy properties need an important qualification: Django\u2019s `cached_property` stores its result directly in `instance.__dict__` on first access ([django/utils/functional.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/utils/functional.py:39)). Consequently, it pays descriptor/computation cost only on the first access per field; warmed accesses are effectively ordinary instance lookups. Lazy initialization can still hurt startup or workloads creating many model/field instances, but it does not recompute millions of times. Ordinary properties such as `db_returning` and `flatchoices` remain recurring costs.\n\nPractical optimizations are:\n\n- Store stable, frequently read values directly during `Field.__init__()`. For example, retain `_unique` for `deconstruct()` while eagerly storing the effective `unique = unique or primary_key`.\n- Initialize values requiring an attached model, such as `cached_col`, once in `contribute_to_class()` after `self.model` and the database table are known.\n- Cache derived values such as `db_returning` when their inputs are immutable after model construction.\n- Cache `flatchoices` only if choices are treated as immutable, and invalidate/recompute it in the `choices` setter; this avoids repeatedly allocating lists.\n- In very hot ORM loops, bind repeatedly used metadata once\u2014such as `field_unique = field.unique`\u2014instead of performing the attribute lookup for every row.\n- Keep lazy initialization for genuinely expensive or rarely used state, especially connection-dependent validators, where eager initialization would increase startup cost or produce incorrect backend-specific values.\n\nThe safest overall strategy is to eagerly materialize cheap, immutable hot-path metadata; retain laziness for expensive or connection-dependent data; and add explicit invalidation wherever mutable inputs such as `choices` are involved.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 8, "relevance": 9, "clarity": 8, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 36782, "output_tokens": 2289, "total_tokens": 39071, "cache_read_tokens": 124928, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 6, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/db/models/fields/__init__.py", "django/utils/functional.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "code-review-graph", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "code-review-graph", "server_name": "crg", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "launch_args": ["serve", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__crg__semantic_search_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__query_graph_tool", "mcp__crg__traverse_graph_tool", "mcp__crg__get_flow_tool", "mcp__crg__list_flows_tool", "mcp__crg__get_affected_flows_tool", "mcp__crg__get_architecture_overview_tool", "mcp__crg__get_bridge_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__get_community_tool", "mcp__crg__list_communities_tool", "mcp__crg__get_hub_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__get_impact_radius_tool", "mcp__crg__get_knowledge_gaps_tool", "mcp__crg__get_minimal_context_tool", "mcp__crg__get_docs_section_tool", "mcp__crg__get_wiki_page_tool", "mcp__crg__get_suggested_questions_tool", "mcp__crg__get_surprising_connections_tool", "mcp__crg__find_large_functions_tool", "mcp__crg__list_graph_stats_tool"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "build", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django", "--embedding-provider", "local", "--embedding-model", "sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["apply_refactor_tool", "build_or_update_graph_tool", "cross_repo_search_tool", "detect_changes_tool", "embed_graph_tool", "find_large_functions_tool", "generate_wiki_tool", "get_affected_flows_tool", "get_architecture_overview_tool", "get_bridge_nodes_tool", "get_community_tool", "get_docs_section_tool", "get_flow_tool", "get_hub_nodes_tool", "get_impact_radius_tool", "get_knowledge_gaps_tool", "get_minimal_context_tool", "get_review_context_tool", "get_suggested_questions_tool", "get_surprising_connections_tool", "get_wiki_page_tool", "list_communities_tool", "list_flows_tool", "list_graph_stats_tool", "list_repos_tool", "query_graph_tool", "refactor_tool", "run_postprocess_tool", "semantic_search_nodes_tool", "traverse_graph_tool"], "served_count": 30, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__crg__get_minimal_context_tool"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"crg": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "code-review-graph", "rc": 0, "seconds": 259.0, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "build", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django", "--embedding-provider", "local", "--embedding-model", "sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 96.59388637542725, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 9.208306550979614, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.08448349999999999, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:15:45.735081+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why is there a cumulative performance impact of the Field class's multiple property accessors and lazy initialization patterns when accessing field attributes millions of times in ORM operations, and how can this be optimized?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 96592, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_031_code-review-graph.json"}
{"task_id": "django_031", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "serena", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The impact is cumulative because ORM work repeatedly crosses Python descriptor boundaries for metadata that is mostly stable. Each individual lookup is cheap, but millions of lookups turn descriptor dispatch, method calls, condition checks, settings lookups, iterator construction, and occasional allocation into measurable CPU time.\n\nConcrete examples:\n\n- [`Field.unique`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:956) computes `self._unique or self.primary_key`.\n- [`Field.db_tablespace`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:960) is a regular property that reevaluates its fallback through `settings.DEFAULT_INDEX_TABLESPACE` every time.\n- [`Field.db_returning`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:964) calls `has_db_default()` on every access.\n- [`Field.flatchoices`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:1124) is more expensive: it traverses choices and allocates a new list on every access.\n- [`Field.get_default()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:1053) performs a method call followed by access to the lazily initialized `_get_default`.\n- [`Field.get_col()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:566) repeatedly examines model metadata before returning `cached_col`.\n\nThis matters in real hot paths. For example, model saving checks `field.db_returning` in [`Model._save_table`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/base.py:1186), field display rendering rebuilds a dictionary from `field.flatchoices` in [`Model._get_FIELD_display`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/base.py:1335), and schema generation accesses `field.db_tablespace` multiple times in [`BaseDatabaseSchemaEditor`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/backends/base/schema.py:368).\n\nThere are two distinct cost profiles:\n\n1. Regular `@property` accessors always invoke descriptor logic and their function bodies. Their cost is paid on every lookup.\n2. Django\u2019s `cached_property` pays descriptor and initialization cost once, then writes the value into `instance.__dict__`. Subsequent lookups are ordinary instance-attribute reads, as shown by [`cached_property.__get__()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/utils/functional.py:39). These are already well optimized for repeated access, although first access can still allocate objects, closures, or lists.\n\nThe best optimizations are therefore:\n\n- Eagerly materialize stable values. Values such as `unique` and potentially `db_returning` can be computed during `Field.__init__()` or when the field is finalized by `contribute_to_class()`, leaving a plain instance attribute for hot reads. Keep `_unique` separately because deconstruction needs the originally supplied argument.\n- Retain `cached_property` for genuinely expensive, rarely used values such as `cached_col`, `error_messages`, `validators`, `_get_default`, and `_db_default_expression`. Eager initialization of all of these would increase model-startup time and memory even when unused.\n- Cache repeated derived collections at the consumer when semantics permit. For fixed choices, cache the flattened tuple or its lookup dictionary instead of repeatedly executing `list(flatten_choices(...))` and `dict(...)`.\n- Hoist repeated access inside loops: read `field.db_tablespace`, `field.flatchoices`, or `field.db_returning` once into a local variable.\n- Add explicit cache invalidation if mutable metadata can change after construction.\n\nThere is an important semantic constraint around choices: [`normalize_choices()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/utils/choices.py:72) deliberately wraps callable choices for lazy evaluation. Such callables may produce dynamic results, so globally caching `flatchoices` would change behavior. Cache only static choices, or invalidate/recompute when choices are callable or reassigned.\n\nLikewise, eagerly storing `db_tablespace` would freeze the current setting and could break code that changes `DEFAULT_INDEX_TABLESPACE` after field creation. A safer optimization is either caching it only after application configuration is finalized or hoisting the property access within schema-generation operations.\n\nIn short: turn stable metadata into plain attributes, preserve one-time `cached_property` initialization for expensive optional metadata, and avoid repeatedly rebuilding dynamic collections. The speedup comes from eliminating tiny per-access costs along the ORM\u2019s multiplicative dimensions: fields \u00d7 rows \u00d7 query/compiler/save operations.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 8, "reasoning": 9, "clarity": 9, "relevance": 9}, "input_tokens": 51453, "output_tokens": 2604, "total_tokens": 54057, "cache_read_tokens": 388096, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 23, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": [], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "serena", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "serena", "server_name": "serena", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/serena.exe", "launch_args": ["start-mcp-server", "--project", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-serena-django-django", "--transport", "stdio", "--enable-web-dashboard", "false", "--enable-gui-log-window", "false"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__get_symbols_overview", "mcp__serena__find_declaration", "mcp__serena__find_implementations", "mcp__serena__find_referencing_symbols", "mcp__serena__get_diagnostics_for_file", "mcp__serena__find_file", "mcp__serena__list_dir", "mcp__serena__read_file"], "index": null, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-serena-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["activate_project", "create_text_file", "delete_memory", "edit_memory", "execute_shell_command", "find_declaration", "find_file", "find_implementations", "find_referencing_symbols", "find_symbol", "get_current_config", "get_diagnostics_for_file", "get_symbols_overview", "initial_instructions", "insert_after_symbol", "insert_before_symbol", "list_dir", "list_memories", "onboarding", "read_file", "read_memory", "rename_memory", "rename_symbol", "replace_content", "replace_in_files", "replace_symbol_body", "safe_delete_symbol", "search_for_pattern", "write_memory"], "served_count": 29, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__serena__initial_instructions", "mcp__serena__activate_project", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_referencing_symbols", "mcp__serena__find_referencing_symbols", "mcp__serena__find_referencing_symbols", "mcp__serena__find_referencing_symbols", "mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"serena": {"ok": 23, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"skipped": "no-index-by-design", "seconds": 0.0}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 110.16065883636475, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 7.625741958618164, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.13886825, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:17:54.599692+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why is there a cumulative performance impact of the Field class's multiple property accessors and lazy initialization patterns when accessing field attributes millions of times in ORM operations, and how can this be optimized?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 110158, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_031_serena.json"}
{"task_id": "django_033", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "c0-bare", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "Migration generation calls `Field.deconstruct()` many times per field, so even fixed-size bookkeeping inside it becomes expensive when multiplied across hundreds of fields.\n\nThe main amplification points are:\n\n- `ModelState.from_model()` clones every local and many-to-many field ([state.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/migrations/state.py:799)). `Field.clone()` performs that clone by calling `self.deconstruct()` and reconstructing the field ([fields/__init__.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:698)).\n- `MigrationAutodetector.generate_altered_fields()` visits every field present in both states and deeply deconstructs both its old and new versions ([autodetector.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/migrations/autodetector.py:1252), [autodetector.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/migrations/autodetector.py:1334)).\n- `deep_deconstruct()` recursively walks every argument and keyword dictionary returned by each field ([autodetector.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/migrations/autodetector.py:74)). Thus, dictionary work inside `Field.deconstruct()` is followed by another traversal of the resulting dictionary.\n- Rename detection can be worse: each added-field candidate is compared with possible removed fields, deconstructing old candidates inside the nested loop ([autodetector.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/migrations/autodetector.py:1062)). With many additions and removals, this can approach quadratic candidate comparisons.\n- When writing the migration, `ModelFieldSerializer.serialize()` calls `deconstruct()` again ([serializer.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/migrations/serializer.py:250)), then iterates and serializes its keyword arguments.\n\nConsequently, an implementation that loops through a dictionary of all possible field options on every call performs all lookups and comparisons even when almost every option has its default value. Repeated `str.replace()` calls for normalizing the field\u2019s import path also scan the entire path and may allocate intermediate strings on every invocation. Each operation is individually small, but the cloning, old/new comparison, rename detection, recursive traversal, and final serialization phases multiply that cost.\n\nThe current source avoids much of this overhead in `Field.deconstruct()`:\n\n- It uses direct conditional checks and only inserts non-default keywords ([fields/__init__.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:647)).\n- It normalizes core field paths using one prefix check followed by `removeprefix()` and `partition()`, rather than several full-string replacements ([fields/__init__.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:629)).\n\nThis does not change the usual linear scaling with the number of fields, but it substantially reduces the per-call constant and temporary allocations. That matters because migration generation may invoke deconstruction several times per field\u2014and potentially many more times during rename-candidate matching.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 8, "completeness": 8, "relevance": 9, "clarity": 8, "reasoning": 8}, "input_tokens": 39279, "output_tokens": 2314, "total_tokens": 41593, "cache_read_tokens": 172032, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 7, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/db/migrations/autodetector.py", "django/db/migrations/serializer.py", "django/db/migrations/state.py", "django/db/migrations/writer.py", "django/db/models/fields/__init__.py", "django\\\\db\\\\migrations\\\\autodetector.py", "django\\\\db\\\\migrations\\\\state.py", "django\\\\db\\\\models\\\\fields\\\\__init__.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "c0-bare", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "c0-bare", "server_name": null, "launch_command": null, "launch_args": null, "served_tools": null, "client_tools": [], "index": null, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-c0-bare-django-django"}, "served_tools": [], "served_count": null, "mcp_tools_issued": [], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": null, "mcp_per_server": {}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 70.18181228637695, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 7.6791815757751465, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.09374274999999999, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:20:03.760374+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why does the deconstruct() method's extensive dictionary iteration and string replacement operations affect migration generation performance when processing models with hundreds of fields?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 70180, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_033_c0-bare.json"}
{"task_id": "django_033", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "repowise", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The performance impact comes from `Field.deconstruct()` being a hot, per-field operation\u2014not from one unusually expensive call.\n\nIn [django/db/models/fields/__init__.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:597), `Field.deconstruct()` must:\n\n- Normalize the field class\u2019s import path.\n- Examine every supported field option\u2014`primary_key`, `max_length`, `default`, `choices`, validators, error messages, and many others.\n- Build a dictionary containing every non-default option.\n\nThese operations are individually cheap, but migration autodetection invokes them repeatedly. In [django/db/migrations/autodetector.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/migrations/autodetector.py:74), `deep_deconstruct()` calls `obj.deconstruct()` and then recursively rebuilds its argument and keyword dictionaries. It is used when comparing fields for additions, alterations, and renames, including both old and new versions of a field. `only_relation_agnostic_fields()` also deconstructs every field while searching for renames.\n\nConsequently, for hundreds of fields:\n\n1. Every field incurs the full set of default checks and import-path string processing.\n2. Every returned `kwargs` dictionary is iterated again recursively by `deep_deconstruct()`.\n3. Old and new model states may cause the same logical field definitions to be deconstructed multiple times.\n4. If a migration is emitted, serialization adds another pass: `ModelFieldSerializer.serialize()` calls the field\u2019s `deconstruct()` again in [django/db/migrations/serializer.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/migrations/serializer.py:250), and `serialize_deconstructed()` sorts and iterates its keyword arguments.\n\nThe basic scaling is linear in the number of fields and their serialized metadata, roughly `O(fields \u00d7 deconstruction passes \u00d7 keyword/metadata size)`. String operations also scan and allocate new strings, while dictionary comprehensions allocate new dictionaries. With a few fields that constant cost is negligible; with hundreds, repeated calls, allocations, recursive traversal, and serialization make it visible during `makemigrations`.\n\nThe current implementation reflects an optimization of this hot path: it uses direct conditional checks instead of constructing and iterating a large defaults dictionary, and uses `startswith()`, `removeprefix()`, and `partition()` to normalize only relevant Django field paths. That reduces constant overhead, even though the migration process must still examine every field.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 8, "relevance": 9, "clarity": 8, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 36047, "output_tokens": 1503, "total_tokens": 37550, "cache_read_tokens": 194816, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 6, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["\\\\db\\\\migrations\\\\writer.py", "django\\\\db\\\\migrations\\\\autodetector.py", "django\\\\db\\\\migrations\\\\serializer.py", "django\\\\db\\\\migrations\\\\writer.py", "django\\\\db\\\\models\\\\fields\\\\__init__.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer"], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "repowise", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "repowise", "server_name": "repowise", "launch_command": "C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/repowise-layerb/.venv/Scripts/repowise.exe", "launch_args": ["mcp", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-repowise-full-django-django", "--transport", "stdio"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer", "mcp__repowise__get_context", "mcp__repowise__get_overview", "mcp__repowise__get_risk", "mcp__repowise__get_symbol", "mcp__repowise__get_why", "mcp__repowise__search_codebase"], "index": null, "shares_index_with": "repowise-full", "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-repowise-full-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["get_answer", "get_change_risk", "get_context", "get_dead_code", "get_health", "get_overview", "get_risk", "get_symbol", "get_why", "list_repos", "search_codebase"], "served_count": 11, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"repowise": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"builtin": "repowise_legacy", "rc": 0, "seconds": 0.0, "index_vector_dim": 1536, "index_embedder_mock": false}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 66.32460761070251, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 7.644585132598877, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.08444075, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:21:21.759415+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why does the deconstruct() method's extensive dictionary iteration and string replacement operations affect migration generation performance when processing models with hundreds of fields?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 66322, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_033_repowise.json"}
{"task_id": "django_033", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "codegraph", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "Migration generation repeatedly invokes `deconstruct()` for every field, so even small per-call overhead becomes significant on very wide models.\n\nThe call path is:\n\n1. [`ModelState.from_model()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/migrations/state.py:799) iterates through `model._meta.local_fields` and `local_many_to_many`.\n2. Each field is cloned with `field.clone()` at lines 810 and 824.\n3. [`Field.clone()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:698) calls `self.deconstruct()`.\n4. [`Field.deconstruct()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:597) computes the portable import path and extracts every non-default constructor argument.\n\nThus, for a model with \\(F\\) fields, all work inside `deconstruct()` runs at least \\(F\\) times\u2014and potentially more during migration comparison and serialization.\n\nHistorically, implementing this extraction by constructing and iterating dictionaries of field attributes/defaults creates several costs per field:\n\n- Allocating temporary dictionaries and tuples.\n- Iterating every possible option even when almost all have default values.\n- Performing dictionary lookups and generic equality checks.\n- Running repeated string `replace()` operations over the complete class import path, each of which scans and allocates a new string.\n\nThose operations are individually small, but hundreds of fields turn them into thousands of dictionary operations, comparisons, string scans, and temporary allocations. Subclass `deconstruct()` implementations can add further work after calling `super().deconstruct()`.\n\nThe current implementation reflects a performance-oriented structure:\n\n- Lines 629\u2013645 use `startswith()`, `removeprefix()`, and `partition()` to normalize the path without a sequence of unconditional replacements.\n- Lines 647\u2013694 use direct attribute checks rather than iterating attribute/default dictionaries.\n- Only non-default values are inserted into the result dictionary.\n\nThis preserves the required output\u2014`(name, path, args, kwargs)`\u2014while reducing generic iteration, lookup, string allocation, and comparison overhead on the migration-generation hot path. The impact is especially visible for models with hundreds of fields because the cost scales linearly with field count and is repeated whenever Django constructs or compares model states.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 6, "completeness": 5, "relevance": 7, "clarity": 8, "reasoning": 6}, "input_tokens": 55930, "output_tokens": 1374, "total_tokens": 57304, "cache_read_tokens": 185344, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 6, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/db/migrations/state.py", "django/db/models/fields/__init__.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "codegraph", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "codegraph", "server_name": "codegraph", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "launch_args": ["serve", "--mcp", "--path", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django", "--no-watch"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "init", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["codegraph_explore"], "served_count": 1, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore", "mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore", "mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"codegraph": {"ok": 3, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "codegraph", "rc": 0, "seconds": 1.1, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "init", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 57.079123735427856, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 8.454952955245972, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.10682050000000001, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:22:40.967833+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why does the deconstruct() method's extensive dictionary iteration and string replacement operations affect migration generation performance when processing models with hundreds of fields?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 57077, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_033_codegraph.json"}
{"task_id": "django_033", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "graphify", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "Migration generation repeatedly deconstructs every field in both the old and new model states. Consequently, even \u201csmall\u201d per-call work becomes significant for models with hundreds of fields.\n\nThe main amplification points are:\n\n- [`Field.deconstruct()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:597) builds the field\u2019s portable import path and keyword arguments. Any implementation that scans a defaults dictionary or repeatedly replaces path substrings pays that cost for every invocation.\n- [`Field.clone()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:698) itself calls `deconstruct()`. [`ModelState.from_model()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/migrations/state.py:799) clones every local and many-to-many field, so state construction already invokes it once per field.\n- [`MigrationAutodetector.deep_deconstruct()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/migrations/autodetector.py:74) invokes `deconstruct()` again and recursively rebuilds every list, tuple, and dictionary in the result. It is used when comparing old and new fields, including the alteration checks around line 1334.\n- Rename detection can be more expensive: around [`generate_renamed_fields()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/migrations/autodetector.py:1050), each newly added field may be compared with multiple removed-field candidates, repeatedly deep-deconstructing candidates.\n- [`ModelState.__eq__()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/migrations/state.py:1006) also deconstructs corresponding fields from both states during equality checks.\n\nThus, iterating a dictionary of defaults makes the normal cost roughly `O(fields \u00d7 defaults \u00d7 number_of_passes)`, while rename candidate comparisons can approach quadratic behavior in the number of candidate fields. Repeated string replacements also allocate a new string on every replacement, adding allocation and garbage-collection pressure.\n\nThe current `Field.deconstruct()` reflects a performance-oriented implementation: lines 629\u2013645 use `startswith()`, `removeprefix()`, and `partition()` for targeted path normalization, while lines 647\u2013694 use direct conditional checks instead of walking a defaults dictionary. This reduces constant work and temporary allocations in a very hot method; multiplied across hundreds of fields and repeated migration-state comparisons, that materially improves migration generation time.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 8, "completeness": 7, "relevance": 9, "clarity": 8, "reasoning": 8}, "input_tokens": 28056, "output_tokens": 1898, "total_tokens": 29954, "cache_read_tokens": 149248, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 7, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/db/migrations/autodetector.py", "django/db/migrations/state.py", "django/db/models/fields/__init__.py", "django\\\\db\\\\migrations\\\\autodetector.py", "django\\\\db\\\\migrations\\\\state.py", "django\\\\db\\\\models\\\\fields\\\\__init__.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "graphify", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "graphify", "server_name": "graphify", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify-mcp.exe", "launch_args": ["--transport", "stdio", "--graph", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django/graphify-out/graph.json"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__graphify__query_graph", "mcp__graphify__get_node", "mcp__graphify__get_neighbors", "mcp__graphify__shortest_path", "mcp__graphify__get_community", "mcp__graphify__god_nodes", "mcp__graphify__graph_stats"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify.exe", "update", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["get_community", "get_neighbors", "get_node", "get_pr_impact", "god_nodes", "graph_stats", "list_prs", "query_graph", "shortest_path", "triage_prs"], "served_count": 10, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__graphify__query_graph", "mcp__graphify__get_node"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"graphify": {"ok": 2, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "graphify", "rc": 0, "seconds": 211.2, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify.exe", "update", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 76.57321643829346, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 8.267950296401978, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.07270599999999999, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:23:46.904444+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why does the deconstruct() method's extensive dictionary iteration and string replacement operations affect migration generation performance when processing models with hundreds of fields?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 76571, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_033_graphify.json"}
{"task_id": "django_033", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "code-review-graph", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "`Field.deconstruct()` is a hot path during migration generation, so small per-call costs become significant on very wide models.\n\nIn [django/db/models/fields/__init__.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:597), `deconstruct()` converts each field into the `(name, path, args, kwargs)` representation used to compare and recreate model state. It is invoked repeatedly:\n\n- `Field.clone()` calls `deconstruct()` at [fields/__init__.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:697).\n- `ModelState.from_model()` clones every local field at [state.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/migrations/state.py:803).\n- Rendering model state clones all stored fields again at [state.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/migrations/state.py:982).\n- `MigrationAutodetector.deep_deconstruct()` recursively deconstructs fields for rename and alteration comparisons at [autodetector.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/migrations/autodetector.py:74), including repeated old/new comparisons around lines 1062\u20131068 and 1334\u20131335.\n\nThe previous implementation created two constant dictionaries and a set on every call: approximately 23 possible keyword defaults, five attribute-name overrides, and the equality-comparison set. It then iterated through every possible option, performed dictionary lookups, called dynamic `getattr()`, and compared values\u2014even though typical fields use almost all defaults and emit very few keywords.\n\nIt also normalized the class path through a seven-branch `startswith()`/`replace()` chain. Each `str.replace()` scans the string and allocates a new one. These operations are individually cheap, but migration detection may deconstruct each field several times. With hundreds of fields, the cost scales roughly with:\n\n`number of fields \u00d7 number of migration-state passes \u00d7 fixed deconstruction work`\n\nThe current implementation addresses this directly:\n\n- It uses explicit attribute checks and only inserts non-default values, avoiding per-call construction and traversal of the defaults/override dictionaries.\n- It computes the path prefix once, then uses `removeprefix()` and `partition()` instead of repeatedly testing and replacing full strings.\n- It retains the same serialized result while reducing allocations, hash lookups, dynamic attribute access, and string scans.\n\nThus, the performance issue is not algorithmic complexity within one field; it is large constant overhead in a very frequently repeated operation, amplified across hundreds of fields and multiple cloning/comparison passes.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 8, "completeness": 6, "relevance": 8, "clarity": 8, "reasoning": 8}, "input_tokens": 36242, "output_tokens": 1758, "total_tokens": 38000, "cache_read_tokens": 166656, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 10, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/db/models/fields/__init__.py", "django\\\\db\\\\migrations\\\\autodetector.py", "django\\\\db\\\\migrations\\\\state.py", "django\\\\db\\\\models\\\\fields\\\\__init__.py", "django\\\\db\\\\models\\\\options.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "code-review-graph", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "code-review-graph", "server_name": "crg", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "launch_args": ["serve", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__crg__semantic_search_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__query_graph_tool", "mcp__crg__traverse_graph_tool", "mcp__crg__get_flow_tool", "mcp__crg__list_flows_tool", "mcp__crg__get_affected_flows_tool", "mcp__crg__get_architecture_overview_tool", "mcp__crg__get_bridge_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__get_community_tool", "mcp__crg__list_communities_tool", "mcp__crg__get_hub_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__get_impact_radius_tool", "mcp__crg__get_knowledge_gaps_tool", "mcp__crg__get_minimal_context_tool", "mcp__crg__get_docs_section_tool", "mcp__crg__get_wiki_page_tool", "mcp__crg__get_suggested_questions_tool", "mcp__crg__get_surprising_connections_tool", "mcp__crg__find_large_functions_tool", "mcp__crg__list_graph_stats_tool"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "build", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django", "--embedding-provider", "local", "--embedding-model", "sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["apply_refactor_tool", "build_or_update_graph_tool", "cross_repo_search_tool", "detect_changes_tool", "embed_graph_tool", "find_large_functions_tool", "generate_wiki_tool", "get_affected_flows_tool", "get_architecture_overview_tool", "get_bridge_nodes_tool", "get_community_tool", "get_docs_section_tool", "get_flow_tool", "get_hub_nodes_tool", "get_impact_radius_tool", "get_knowledge_gaps_tool", "get_minimal_context_tool", "get_review_context_tool", "get_suggested_questions_tool", "get_surprising_connections_tool", "get_wiki_page_tool", "list_communities_tool", "list_flows_tool", "list_graph_stats_tool", "list_repos_tool", "query_graph_tool", "refactor_tool", "run_postprocess_tool", "semantic_search_nodes_tool", "traverse_graph_tool"], "served_count": 30, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__crg__semantic_search_nodes_tool"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"crg": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "code-review-graph", "rc": 0, "seconds": 259.0, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "build", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django", "--embedding-provider", "local", "--embedding-model", "sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 87.12171697616577, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 7.809329032897949, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.0837145, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:25:16.389575+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why does the deconstruct() method's extensive dictionary iteration and string replacement operations affect migration generation performance when processing models with hundreds of fields?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 87118, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_033_code-review-graph.json"}
{"task_id": "django_033", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "serena", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "Migration generation invokes `Field.deconstruct()` repeatedly for every field, so small per-call inefficiencies multiply dramatically on wide models.\n\nIn [`django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:597`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:597), `Field.deconstruct()` must:\n\n- Produce the portable import path.\n- Inspect roughly two dozen field attributes.\n- Retain only values differing from their defaults.\n- Construct the keyword dictionary used to recreate the field.\n\nA generic implementation based on iterating dictionaries of possible arguments incurs, for every field, iterator overhead, dictionary lookups, attribute access, and default comparisons\u2014even though most fields use defaults. Likewise, repeatedly calling `str.replace()` to normalize the class path scans the whole path and creates new strings, including when the target substring is absent.\n\nThe current code reduces those costs:\n\n- Path normalization uses one `startswith()` test followed by targeted `removeprefix()` and `partition()` operations.\n- Keyword extraction uses direct, fixed conditionals such as `if self.max_length is not None`, adding only non-default values instead of traversing a metadata dictionary.\n\nThis matters because deconstruction is not necessarily performed only once per field:\n\n- [`Field.clone()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:698) calls it whenever migration state clones a field.\n- [`MigrationAutodetector.deep_deconstruct()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/migrations/autodetector.py:99) calls it while recursively comparing migration values.\n- [`ModelState.__eq__()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/migrations/state.py:1011) deconstructs both old and new versions of every paired field.\n- [`ModelFieldSerializer.serialize()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/migrations/serializer.py:251) calls it again when writing migration code.\n\nThus, the basic scaling is approximately `O(F \u00d7 P)` where `F` is the number of fields and `P` is the number of properties/path operations examined per deconstruction. `P` is fixed, so this is asymptotically linear, but migration comparison, cloning, and serialization repeat that work. With hundreds of fields, thousands of unnecessary dictionary operations, string scans, and temporary allocations accumulate into noticeable migration-generation latency.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 8, "completeness": 6, "reasoning": 7, "clarity": 8, "relevance": 8}, "input_tokens": 50321, "output_tokens": 1485, "total_tokens": 51806, "cache_read_tokens": 154624, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 6, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": [], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "serena", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "serena", "server_name": "serena", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/serena.exe", "launch_args": ["start-mcp-server", "--project", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-serena-django-django", "--transport", "stdio", "--enable-web-dashboard", "false", "--enable-gui-log-window", "false"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__get_symbols_overview", "mcp__serena__find_declaration", "mcp__serena__find_implementations", "mcp__serena__find_referencing_symbols", "mcp__serena__get_diagnostics_for_file", "mcp__serena__find_file", "mcp__serena__list_dir", "mcp__serena__read_file"], "index": null, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-serena-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["activate_project", "create_text_file", "delete_memory", "edit_memory", "execute_shell_command", "find_declaration", "find_file", "find_implementations", "find_referencing_symbols", "find_symbol", "get_current_config", "get_diagnostics_for_file", "get_symbols_overview", "initial_instructions", "insert_after_symbol", "insert_before_symbol", "list_dir", "list_memories", "onboarding", "read_file", "read_memory", "rename_memory", "rename_symbol", "replace_content", "replace_in_files", "replace_symbol_body", "safe_delete_symbol", "search_for_pattern", "write_memory"], "served_count": 29, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__serena__initial_instructions", "mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__find_symbol"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"serena": {"ok": 6, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"skipped": "no-index-by-design", "seconds": 0.0}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 61.316280364990234, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 9.820781946182251, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.09707925, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:27:10.153415+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why does the deconstruct() method's extensive dictionary iteration and string replacement operations affect migration generation performance when processing models with hundreds of fields?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 61314, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_033_serena.json"}
{"task_id": "django_034", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "c0-bare", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "`test_url_asvar03` verifies the URL tag\u2019s graceful-failure behavior when the optional `as` assignment syntax is used.\n\nIn [test_url.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_url.py:239), the template is:\n\n```django\n{% url \"no_such_view\" as url %}{{ url }}\n```\n\nBecause `no_such_view` does not exist, reversing it normally raises `NoReverseMatch`, as explicitly tested by `test_url_fail02` in the same file. With `as url`, however, the expected rendered output is `\"\"`.\n\nThis corresponds directly to `URLNode.render()` in [defaulttags.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/template/defaulttags.py:494):\n\n- It initializes `url` to `\"\"`.\n- It attempts `reverse(...)`.\n- If reversal raises `NoReverseMatch`, it re-raises only when `self.asvar is None`.\n- When `asvar` is present, it assigns the empty string to the context variable and emits nothing itself.\n\nThe parser recognizes the trailing `as url` and passes `url` as `asvar` to `URLNode` in [defaulttags.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/template/defaulttags.py:1560).\n\nTherefore, the test ensures that assignment form can be used for optional links: a missing URL pattern does not abort template rendering, and the assigned variable evaluates as empty. It complements `test_url_asvar02`, which confirms that successful reversal assigns `/` to the variable.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 17085, "output_tokens": 954, "total_tokens": 18039, "cache_read_tokens": 68352, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 4, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/template/defaulttags.py", "django\\\\template\\\\defaulttags.py", "tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_url.py", "tests\\\\template_tests\\\\syntax_tests\\\\test_url.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "c0-bare", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "c0-bare", "server_name": null, "launch_command": null, "launch_args": null, "served_tools": null, "client_tools": [], "index": null, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-c0-bare-django-django"}, "served_tools": [], "served_count": null, "mcp_tools_issued": [], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": null, "mcp_per_server": {}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 41.56323170661926, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 7.336425304412842, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.039440249999999996, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:28:32.557659+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why is the purpose of the test_url_asvar03 function in validating the URL template tag's behavior when using the 'as' variable assignment syntax?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 41562, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_034_c0-bare.json"}
{"task_id": "django_034", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "repowise", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "`test_url_asvar03` verifies the failure-handling contract of `{% url ... as variable %}`, not merely that assignment mode produces no direct output.\n\nIn [test_url.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_url.py:239), the template is:\n\n```django\n{% url \"no_such_view\" as url %}{{ url }}\n```\n\nThe test asserts that rendering returns `\"\"`. This confirms that when the named view cannot be reversed:\n\n- `NoReverseMatch` is suppressed because `as url` was supplied.\n- `url` is assigned the empty string.\n- The tag itself emits nothing.\n- Consequently, rendering `{{ url }}` also emits nothing.\n\nThis follows `URLNode.render()` in [defaulttags.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/template/defaulttags.py:494): it initializes `url = \"\"`, catches `NoReverseMatch`, and only re-raises when `self.asvar is None`. With an assignment variable, it stores that empty value in `context[self.asvar]` and returns `\"\"` at lines 516\u2013518.\n\nThe neighboring tests clarify the distinction:\n\n- `test_url_asvar01` checks that assignment syntax itself emits nothing.\n- `test_url_asvar02` checks that a successfully reversed URL is assigned and can subsequently be rendered.\n- `test_url_asvar03` checks that an unresolvable URL safely becomes an empty assigned value instead of raising an exception.\n\nThus, its specific purpose is to ensure `{% url ... as var %}` can be used as a graceful, non-throwing URL lookup when the route may not exist.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 8, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 21013, "output_tokens": 737, "total_tokens": 21750, "cache_read_tokens": 71680, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 2, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/template/defaulttags.py", "tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_url.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer"], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "repowise", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "repowise", "server_name": "repowise", "launch_command": "C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/repowise-layerb/.venv/Scripts/repowise.exe", "launch_args": ["mcp", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-repowise-full-django-django", "--transport", "stdio"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer", "mcp__repowise__get_context", "mcp__repowise__get_overview", "mcp__repowise__get_risk", "mcp__repowise__get_symbol", "mcp__repowise__get_why", "mcp__repowise__search_codebase"], "index": null, "shares_index_with": "repowise-full", "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-repowise-full-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["get_answer", "get_change_risk", "get_context", "get_dead_code", "get_health", "get_overview", "get_risk", "get_symbol", "get_why", "list_repos", "search_codebase"], "served_count": 11, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__repowise__get_answer"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"repowise": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"builtin": "repowise_legacy", "rc": 0, "seconds": 0.0, "index_vector_dim": 1536, "index_embedder_mock": false}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 43.31852722167969, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 7.468753814697266, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.04259625, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:29:21.681717+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why is the purpose of the test_url_asvar03 function in validating the URL template tag's behavior when using the 'as' variable assignment syntax?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 43316, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_034_repowise.json"}
{"task_id": "django_034", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "codegraph", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "`test_url_asvar03` verifies the URL tag\u2019s failure behavior when the optional `as` assignment syntax is used.\n\nIn [tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_url.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_url.py:239), the template is:\n\n```django\n{% url \"no_such_view\" as url %}{{ url }}\n```\n\nBecause `\"no_such_view\"` cannot be reversed, the test expects an empty rendered string instead of a `NoReverseMatch` exception.\n\nThis validates the special handling in [django/template/defaulttags.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/template/defaulttags.py:494):\n\n- `URLNode.render()` initially sets `url = \"\"`.\n- If `reverse()` raises `NoReverseMatch`, it re-raises only when no `as` variable was supplied.\n- With `as url`, it assigns the empty string to `context[\"url\"]` and emits nothing itself.\n\nThus, the test confirms that `{% url ... as variable %}` supports optional URL lookup: an unresolved route safely becomes an empty variable, allowing templates to conditionally use it without rendering failure. The neighboring `test_url_asvar02` complements it by confirming that a successfully resolved URL is assigned and can be rendered.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 8, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 16465, "output_tokens": 701, "total_tokens": 17166, "cache_read_tokens": 83712, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 3, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": [], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "codegraph", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "codegraph", "server_name": "codegraph", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "launch_args": ["serve", "--mcp", "--path", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django", "--no-watch"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "init", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["codegraph_explore"], "served_count": 1, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore", "mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"codegraph": {"ok": 2, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "codegraph", "rc": 0, "seconds": 1.1, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "init", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 37.432437896728516, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 7.5007219314575195, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.03805525, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:30:16.846907+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why is the purpose of the test_url_asvar03 function in validating the URL template tag's behavior when using the 'as' variable assignment syntax?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 37430, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_034_codegraph.json"}
{"task_id": "django_034", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "graphify", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "`test_url_asvar03()` verifies the failure behavior of the URL tag\u2019s assignment form:\n\n```django\n{% url \"no_such_view\" as url %}{{ url }}\n```\n\nBecause `\"no_such_view\"` cannot be reversed, `URLNode.render()` catches `NoReverseMatch`. When an `as` variable is present, it suppresses the exception, assigns an empty string to `url`, and emits nothing. Therefore, rendering `{{ url }}` also produces `\"\"`.\n\nThis confirms that `{% url ... as var %}` supports optional URL lookup: an unresolved route fails silently instead of raising `NoReverseMatch`. See [test_url.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_url.py:239) and [defaulttags.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/template/defaulttags.py:494).", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 7, "relevance": 9, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 8}, "input_tokens": 14252, "output_tokens": 677, "total_tokens": 14929, "cache_read_tokens": 82432, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 4, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/template/defaulttags.py", "tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_url.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "graphify", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "graphify", "server_name": "graphify", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify-mcp.exe", "launch_args": ["--transport", "stdio", "--graph", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django/graphify-out/graph.json"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__graphify__query_graph", "mcp__graphify__get_node", "mcp__graphify__get_neighbors", "mcp__graphify__shortest_path", "mcp__graphify__get_community", "mcp__graphify__god_nodes", "mcp__graphify__graph_stats"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify.exe", "update", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["get_community", "get_neighbors", "get_node", "get_pr_impact", "god_nodes", "graph_stats", "list_prs", "query_graph", "shortest_path", "triage_prs"], "served_count": 10, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__graphify__query_graph", "mcp__graphify__get_node"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"graphify": {"ok": 2, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "graphify", "rc": 0, "seconds": 211.2, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify.exe", "update", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 37.17732620239258, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 6.7668890953063965, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.034889, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:31:02.178420+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Why is the purpose of the test_url_asvar03 function in validating the URL template tag's behavior when using the 'as' variable assignment syntax?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 37175, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_034_graphify.json"}
{"task_id": "django_034", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "code-review-graph", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "`test_url_asvar03` verifies the URL tag\u2019s failure-tolerant behavior when using `as` assignment.\n\nIn [tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_url.py:239](C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django\\tests\\template_tests\\syntax_tests\\test_url.py:239), the template tries to reverse a nonexistent view:\n\n```django\n{% url \"no_such_view\" as url %}{{ url }}\n```\n\nThe test expects an empty string instead of a `NoReverseMatch` exception.\n\nThis validates the special handling in `URLNode.render()` at [django/template/defaulttags.py:507](C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django\\django\\template\\defaulttags.py:507):\n\n- The URL starts as `\"\"`.\n- `reverse()` fails for `\"no_such_view\"`.\n- Because `self.asvar` is set, `NoReverseMatch` is suppressed.\n- The empty URL is assigned to `context[\"url\"]`.\n- The tag itself emits nothing, and `{{ url }}` consequently renders nothing.\n\nThus, the test ensures that `{% url ... as variable %}` can be used for optional links: an unresolvable URL produces an empty assigned value without aborting template rendering. Without `as`, the same resolution failure would be re-raised.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 8, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 20781, "output_tokens": 873, "total_tokens": 21654, "cache_read_tokens": 139520, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 5, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/template/defaulttags.py", "tests/template_tests/syntax_tests/test_url.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "code-review-graph", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "code-review-graph", "s