9+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Where does the cascade deletion triggered by the ForeignKey relationship in silly_tribble propagate through the data control flow when a referenced Tribble instance is deleted?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 105568, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_040_repowise.json"}
{"task_id": "django_040", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "codegraph", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The cascade originates from this test model:\n\n- [`tests/migrations/migrations_test_apps/unmigrated_app/models.py:4`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/tests/migrations/migrations_test_apps/unmigrated_app/models.py:4) defines `SillyModel`.\n- Its `silly_tribble` field is a `ForeignKey(\"migrations.Tribble\", models.CASCADE)` at line 6.\n- `Tribble` itself is created by [`tests/migrations/test_migrations/0001_initial.py:18`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/tests/migrations/test_migrations/0001_initial.py:18).\n\nThe deletion flow is:\n\n1. Deleting a `Tribble`\n\n   `Tribble.delete()` enters [`Model.delete()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/base.py:1314). It selects the write database, creates a `Collector`, calls `collector.collect([tribble])`, and then `collector.delete()`.\n\n   A queryset deletion follows the equivalent path through [`QuerySet.delete()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/query.py:1308).\n\n2. Discovering the reverse relationship\n\n   [`Collector.collect()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:271) adds the `Tribble` to `Collector.data`, then iterates `get_candidate_relations_to_delete(model._meta)` at line 336.\n\n   [`get_candidate_relations_to_delete()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:106) returns auto-created reverse one-to-many/one-to-one relations. Thus, although `silly_tribble` is declared on `SillyModel`, deletion discovers it through the reverse relation attached to `Tribble`.\n\n3. Selecting `CASCADE`\n\n   For that reverse relation, `Collector.collect()` obtains:\n\n   ```python\n   field = related.field                 # SillyModel.silly_tribble\n   on_delete = field.remote_field.on_delete  # CASCADE\n   related_model = related.related_model     # SillyModel\n   ```\n\n   It constructs a related queryset using [`Collector.related_objects()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:433), effectively:\n\n   ```python\n   SillyModel.objects.filter(silly_tribble__in=[deleted_tribble])\n   ```\n\n4. Propagating to `SillyModel`\n\n   There are two collector branches:\n\n   - Normally, `on_delete(...)` invokes [`CASCADE()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:22), which recursively calls `collector.collect(sub_objs, source=Tribble, source_attr=\"silly_tribble\", nullable=False)`. `Collector.add()` records a dependency requiring `SillyModel` rows to be removed before their `Tribble`.\n   - For this simple `SillyModel`, the collector can ordinarily fast-delete it because it has no further cascades, parents, or deletion signal listeners. In that case, lines 353\u2013398 put the related `SillyModel` queryset in `Collector.fast_deletes`, avoiding object materialization and recursive collection. This is still the implementation of the `CASCADE` semantics.\n\n5. Executing the deletion\n\n   [`Collector.delete()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:466) sorts dependencies and opens a transaction.\n\n   For the likely fast-delete branch, it deletes matching `SillyModel` rows first through `qs._raw_delete()` at lines 500\u2013504. [`QuerySet._raw_delete()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/query.py:1347) converts the queryset to a `DeleteQuery` and executes it directly.\n\n   Collected objects are otherwise deleted in dependency order with `DeleteQuery.delete_batch()` at lines 532\u2013547. [`DeleteQuery.delete_batch()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/sql/subqueries.py:27) generates primary-key-batched SQL deletes.\n\nSo the effective propagation is:\n\n```text\nTribble.delete()\n  \u2192 Collector.collect(Tribble)\n  \u2192 reverse relation for SillyModel.silly_tribble\n  \u2192 CASCADE semantics\n  \u2192 related SillyModel queryset\n  \u2192 fast_deletes (normally) or recursive Collector.collect()\n  \u2192 DELETE SillyModel rows\n  \u2192 DELETE Tribble row\n```\n\nThe cascade stops at `SillyModel` unless that model gains additional cascade-capable reverse relations or related private fields.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 33861, "output_tokens": 2558, "total_tokens": 36419, "cache_read_tokens": 171776, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 7, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/db/models/base.py", "django/db/models/deletion.py", "django/db/models/query.py", "django/db/models/sql/subqueries.py", "tests/migrations/migrations_test_apps/unmigrated_app/models.py", "tests/migrations/test_migrations/0001_initial.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "codegraph", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "codegraph", "server_name": "codegraph", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "launch_args": ["serve", "--mcp", "--path", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django", "--no-watch"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "init", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["codegraph_explore"], "served_count": 1, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__codegraph__codegraph_explore"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"codegraph": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "codegraph", "rc": 0, "seconds": 1.1, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm/codegraph.cmd", "init", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-codegraph-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 94.74831986427307, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 7.422478914260864, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.08937824999999999, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:37:22.889861+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Where does the cascade deletion triggered by the ForeignKey relationship in silly_tribble propagate through the data control flow when a referenced Tribble instance is deleted?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 94746, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_040_codegraph.json"}
{"task_id": "django_040", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "graphify", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The declared cascade points from `Tribble` to dependent `SillyModel` rows:\n\n`Tribble.delete()` \u2192 deletion collector \u2192 reverse `silly_tribble` relation \u2192 matching `SillyModel` objects \u2192 `CASCADE()` \u2192 recursive collection \u2192 SQL deletion.\n\nConcretely:\n\n1. [`SillyModel.silly_tribble`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/tests/migrations/migrations_test_apps/unmigrated_app/models.py:6) declares:\n\n   ```python\n   silly_tribble = models.ForeignKey(\"migrations.Tribble\", models.CASCADE)\n   ```\n\n   Thus deleting the referenced `Tribble` is intended to delete every `SillyModel` whose `silly_tribble_id` matches it.\n\n2. An instance deletion enters [`Model.delete()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/models/base.py:1314). It selects the write database, constructs a `Collector`, calls `collector.collect([tribble])`, then `collector.delete()`. A queryset deletion takes the equivalent route through [`QuerySet.delete()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/models/query.py:1308).\n\n3. [`Collector.collect()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:271) inspects reverse one-to-many and one-to-one relationships returned by [`get_candidate_relations_to_delete()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:106). For the reverse relation created by `silly_tribble`, it obtains `models.CASCADE` from `field.remote_field.on_delete`.\n\n4. [`Collector.related_objects()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:433) builds the equivalent of:\n\n   ```python\n   SillyModel._base_manager.using(database).filter(\n       silly_tribble__in=deleted_tribbles\n   )\n   ```\n\n5. The collector invokes [`CASCADE()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:22), which recursively calls `collector.collect()` on those `SillyModel` objects. Collection could continue through any cascade relationships pointing at `SillyModel`; none are declared in this model, so this branch terminates there.\n\n6. [`Collector.delete()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:466) sends `pre_delete`, deletes collected dependents and the `Tribble` in dependency-safe order using `sql.DeleteQuery.delete_batch()`, then sends `post_delete`.\n\nThere is an important repository-specific caveat: `Tribble` exists only as a historical migration model\u2014for example in [`0001_initial.py`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/tests/migrations/test_migrations/0001_initial.py:19)\u2014and not in the global runtime app registry. The comments in [`test_commands.py`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-graphify-django-django/tests/migrations/test_commands.py:732) explicitly say that `silly_tribble` remains a dangling lazy reference and remove its pending registry operation. Therefore, in this particular test configuration there is no live global `Tribble` model whose instance deletion actually traverses this relationship. The propagation above occurs only in an app/model state where that string reference has been resolved; migration-level `DeleteModel(\"Tribble\")` is schema-state handling, not an invocation of the instance deletion collector.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 8, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 9, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 41893, "output_tokens": 1910, "total_tokens": 43803, "cache_read_tokens": 182016, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 6, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/db/models/base.py", "django/db/models/deletion.py", "django/db/models/query.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "graphify", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "graphify", "server_name": "graphify", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify-mcp.exe", "launch_args": ["--transport", "stdio", "--graph", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django/graphify-out/graph.json"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__graphify__query_graph", "mcp__graphify__get_node", "mcp__graphify__get_neighbors", "mcp__graphify__shortest_path", "mcp__graphify__get_community", "mcp__graphify__god_nodes", "mcp__graphify__graph_stats"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify.exe", "update", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["get_community", "get_neighbors", "get_node", "get_pr_impact", "god_nodes", "graph_stats", "list_prs", "query_graph", "shortest_path", "triage_prs"], "served_count": 10, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__graphify__query_graph"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"graphify": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "graphify", "rc": 0, "seconds": 211.2, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/graphify.exe", "update", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-graphify-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 86.68470120429993, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 7.608079671859741, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.09421825, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:39:05.464205+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Where does the cascade deletion triggered by the ForeignKey relationship in silly_tribble propagate through the data control flow when a referenced Tribble instance is deleted?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 86682, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_040_graphify.json"}
{"task_id": "django_040", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "code-review-graph", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The cascade starts at [`SillyModel.silly_tribble`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/tests/migrations/migrations_test_apps/unmigrated_app/models.py:6):\n\n```python\nsilly_tribble = models.ForeignKey(\"migrations.Tribble\", models.CASCADE)\n```\n\nWhen the referenced `Tribble` is deleted, control flows as follows:\n\n1. `Tribble.delete()` enters [`Model.delete()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/base.py:1314). QuerySet deletion similarly enters [`QuerySet.delete()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/query.py:1308). Both create a `Collector`, collect the target, and then call `Collector.delete()`.\n\n2. [`Collector.collect()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:271) inspects `Tribble._meta` through `get_candidate_relations_to_delete()`. This exposes the reverse one-to-many relation from `Tribble` to `SillyModel`.\n\n3. For that relation, `collect()` reads `silly_tribble.remote_field.on_delete`, which is `CASCADE`, and constructs a `SillyModel` queryset through [`Collector.related_objects()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:433), effectively:\n\n```python\nSillyModel._base_manager.filter(silly_tribble__in=deleted_tribbles)\n```\n\n4. [`CASCADE()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:22) recursively calls `collector.collect()` with those `SillyModel` rows. Because `silly_tribble` is non-nullable, [`Collector.add()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:141) records the deletion dependency `Tribble \u2192 SillyModel`, ensuring dependent rows are removed first.\n\n5. The recursion continues through any cascade-capable reverse relations belonging to each collected `SillyModel`. Thus, the cascade propagates transitively through the same `collect()` \u2192 `related_objects()` \u2192 `on_delete()` loop.\n\n6. Finally, [`Collector.delete()`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-code-review-graph-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:466) sorts by those dependencies, sends `pre_delete`, deletes eligible fast-delete querysets or issues `DeleteQuery.delete_batch()` calls, and sends `post_delete`. The collected `SillyModel` rows are deleted before the original `Tribble`.\n\nSo the concrete propagation is:\n\n```text\nTribble.delete()\n  \u2192 Collector.collect(Tribble)\n  \u2192 reverse relation: SillyModel.silly_tribble\n  \u2192 Collector.related_objects(SillyModel, ...)\n  \u2192 CASCADE()\n  \u2192 Collector.collect(matching SillyModel rows)\n  \u2192 Collector.delete()\n  \u2192 DELETE SillyModel rows\n  \u2192 DELETE Tribble row\n```", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 26042, "output_tokens": 1963, "total_tokens": 28005, "cache_read_tokens": 194304, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 10, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["django/db/models/base.py", "django/db/models/deletion.py", "django/db/models/fields/related.py", "django/db/models/query.py", "tests/migrations/migrations_test_apps/migrated_app/migrations/0001_initial.py", "tests/migrations/migrations_test_apps/unmigrated_app/models.py", "tests/migrations/test_commands.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "code-review-graph", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "code-review-graph", "server_name": "crg", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "launch_args": ["serve", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__crg__semantic_search_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__query_graph_tool", "mcp__crg__traverse_graph_tool", "mcp__crg__get_flow_tool", "mcp__crg__list_flows_tool", "mcp__crg__get_affected_flows_tool", "mcp__crg__get_architecture_overview_tool", "mcp__crg__get_bridge_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__get_community_tool", "mcp__crg__list_communities_tool", "mcp__crg__get_hub_nodes_tool", "mcp__crg__get_impact_radius_tool", "mcp__crg__get_knowledge_gaps_tool", "mcp__crg__get_minimal_context_tool", "mcp__crg__get_docs_section_tool", "mcp__crg__get_wiki_page_tool", "mcp__crg__get_suggested_questions_tool", "mcp__crg__get_surprising_connections_tool", "mcp__crg__find_large_functions_tool", "mcp__crg__list_graph_stats_tool"], "index": {"command": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "build", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django", "--embedding-provider", "local", "--embedding-model", "sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2"], "timeout_seconds": 10800}, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["apply_refactor_tool", "build_or_update_graph_tool", "cross_repo_search_tool", "detect_changes_tool", "embed_graph_tool", "find_large_functions_tool", "generate_wiki_tool", "get_affected_flows_tool", "get_architecture_overview_tool", "get_bridge_nodes_tool", "get_community_tool", "get_docs_section_tool", "get_flow_tool", "get_hub_nodes_tool", "get_impact_radius_tool", "get_knowledge_gaps_tool", "get_minimal_context_tool", "get_review_context_tool", "get_suggested_questions_tool", "get_surprising_connections_tool", "get_wiki_page_tool", "list_communities_tool", "list_flows_tool", "list_graph_stats_tool", "list_repos_tool", "query_graph_tool", "refactor_tool", "run_postprocess_tool", "semantic_search_nodes_tool", "traverse_graph_tool"], "served_count": 30, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__crg__semantic_search_nodes_tool"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"crg": {"ok": 1, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"arm": "code-review-graph", "rc": 0, "seconds": 259.0, "argv": ["C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/code-review-graph.exe", "build", "--repo", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django", "--embedding-provider", "local", "--embedding-model", "sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2"], "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-code-review-graph-django-django"}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 88.58771204948425, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 7.722456932067871, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.0764705, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:40:44.315820+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Where does the cascade deletion triggered by the ForeignKey relationship in silly_tribble propagate through the data control flow when a referenced Tribble instance is deleted?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 88585, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_040_code-review-graph.json"}
{"task_id": "django_040", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "serena", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The cascade is implemented by Django\u2019s Python-level deletion collector. For a resolved `Tribble` model instance, the control flow is:\n\n1. The relationship is declared in [models.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/tests/migrations/migrations_test_apps/unmigrated_app/models.py:5):\n\n   ```python\n   silly_tribble = models.ForeignKey(\"migrations.Tribble\", models.CASCADE)\n   ```\n\n   Thus, deleting a `Tribble` propagates in the reverse direction to every `SillyModel` whose `silly_tribble_id` references it.\n\n2. Deletion enters through either:\n\n   - `Model.delete()` in [base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/base.py:1320), or\n   - `QuerySet.delete()` in [query.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/query.py:1329).\n\n   Both create a `Collector`, call `collector.collect(...)`, and then `collector.delete()`.\n\n3. `Collector.collect()` in [deletion.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:271) adds the `Tribble` to `Collector.data` and examines reverse one-to-many/one-to-one relations returned by `get_candidate_relations_to_delete()`.\n\n4. For the reverse relation created from `SillyModel.silly_tribble`, it obtains:\n\n   ```python\n   field = related.field\n   on_delete = field.remote_field.on_delete\n   ```\n\n   Here, `on_delete` is `CASCADE`. `Collector.related_objects()` in [deletion.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:433) builds the equivalent of:\n\n   ```python\n   SillyModel._base_manager.filter(silly_tribble__in=deleted_tribbles)\n   ```\n\n5. The collector invokes `CASCADE()` in [deletion.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:21). It recursively calls:\n\n   ```python\n   collector.collect(\n       sub_objs,\n       source=field.remote_field.model,\n       source_attr=field.name,\n       nullable=field.null,\n       fail_on_restricted=False,\n   )\n   ```\n\n   Consequently, matching `SillyModel` instances are added to `Collector.data`. Since this FK is non-nullable by default, `Collector.add()` also records a dependency so the referencing `SillyModel` rows are deleted before the referenced `Tribble`.\n\n6. `Collector.delete()` in [deletion.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/deletion.py:466) sorts the dependency graph, sends `pre_delete`, executes fast deletes where possible, and otherwise passes each model\u2019s primary keys to `DeleteQuery.delete_batch()`. That method in [subqueries.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/django/db/models/sql/subqueries.py:27) issues the actual SQL deletes. `post_delete` is then sent.\n\nSo the effective propagation is:\n\n```text\nTribble.delete()\n  \u2192 Collector.collect(Tribble)\n  \u2192 reverse silly_tribble relation\n  \u2192 query matching SillyModel rows\n  \u2192 CASCADE()\n  \u2192 Collector.collect(SillyModel rows)\n  \u2192 Collector.delete()\n  \u2192 DELETE SillyModel rows\n  \u2192 DELETE Tribble row\n```\n\nThere is an important repository-specific caveat: `Tribble` is created only in migration files such as [0001_initial.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/tests/migrations/test_migrations/0001_initial.py:18), not as a normal runtime model. Comments in [test_commands.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-serena-django-django/tests/migrations/test_commands.py:731) explicitly say the global app registry never sees it, leaving `SillyModel.silly_tribble` dangling. Therefore, this cascade path operates when `Tribble` and the relation are resolved in a migration/historical app registry; it cannot be initiated through an ordinary globally registered runtime `Tribble` class in this test app.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 34083, "output_tokens": 2172, "total_tokens": 36255, "cache_read_tokens": 178944, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 14, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": [], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "serena", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "serena", "server_name": "serena", "launch_command": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\.local\\bin/serena.exe", "launch_args": ["start-mcp-server", "--project", "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-serena-django-django", "--transport", "stdio", "--enable-web-dashboard", "false", "--enable-gui-log-window", "false"], "served_tools": null, "client_tools": ["mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__get_symbols_overview", "mcp__serena__find_declaration", "mcp__serena__find_implementations", "mcp__serena__find_referencing_symbols", "mcp__serena__get_diagnostics_for_file", "mcp__serena__find_file", "mcp__serena__list_dir", "mcp__serena__read_file"], "index": null, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-serena-django-django"}, "served_tools": ["activate_project", "create_text_file", "delete_memory", "edit_memory", "execute_shell_command", "find_declaration", "find_file", "find_implementations", "find_referencing_symbols", "find_symbol", "get_current_config", "get_diagnostics_for_file", "get_symbols_overview", "initial_instructions", "insert_after_symbol", "insert_before_symbol", "list_dir", "list_memories", "onboarding", "read_file", "read_memory", "rename_memory", "rename_symbol", "replace_content", "replace_in_files", "replace_symbol_body", "safe_delete_symbol", "search_for_pattern", "write_memory"], "served_count": 29, "mcp_tools_issued": ["mcp__serena__initial_instructions", "mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__search_for_pattern", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol", "mcp__serena__find_symbol"], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": true, "mcp_per_server": {"serena": {"ok": 14, "error": 0}}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {"skipped": "no-index-by-design", "seconds": 0.0}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 76.17310357093811, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 8.311132669448853, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.08669175, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:42:40.021532+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Where does the cascade deletion triggered by the ForeignKey relationship in silly_tribble propagate through the data control flow when a referenced Tribble instance is deleted?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 76171, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_040_serena.json"}
{"task_id": "django_044", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "c0-bare", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The behavior is split between backend connection features, backend type mappings/operations, and `DurationField` itself.\n\n### Native-duration capability\n\nThe default feature flag is defined in [`django/db/backends/base/features.py:85`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/backends/base/features.py:85):\n\n```python\nhas_native_duration_field = False\n```\n\nOnly the native-duration backends override it:\n\n- PostgreSQL: [`django/db/backends/postgresql/features.py:17`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/backends/postgresql/features.py:17)\n- Oracle: [`django/db/backends/oracle/features.py:28`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/backends/oracle/features.py:28)\n\nBoth set it to `True`. MySQL and SQLite inherit the default `False`.\n\nEach backend\u2019s `DatabaseWrapper` assigns its `DatabaseFeatures` implementation through `features_class`; `BaseDatabaseWrapper.__init__()` instantiates it as `self.features` in [`django/db/backends/base/base.py:116`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/backends/base/base.py:116). Thus code checks the selected connection with:\n\n```python\nconnection.features.has_native_duration_field\n```\n\n### Resolving the physical database type\n\n`DurationField.get_internal_type()` returns `\"DurationField\"` in [`django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:1943`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:1943).\n\nThe inherited `Field.db_type()` then looks that string up in the current connection\u2019s backend-specific `data_types` mapping at [`django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:889`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:889). The mappings are:\n\n- PostgreSQL \u2192 `interval` in [`django/db/backends/postgresql/base.py:115`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/backends/postgresql/base.py:115)\n- Oracle \u2192 `INTERVAL DAY(9) TO SECOND(6)` in [`django/db/backends/oracle/base.py:135`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/backends/oracle/base.py:135)\n- MySQL \u2192 `bigint` in [`django/db/backends/mysql/base.py:120`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/backends/mysql/base.py:120)\n- SQLite \u2192 `bigint` in [`django/db/backends/sqlite3/base.py:75`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/backends/sqlite3/base.py:75)\n\n### Value adaptation and conversion\n\nWrites pass through `DurationField.get_db_prep_value()` at [`django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:1965`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:1965), which delegates to:\n\n```python\nconnection.ops.adapt_durationfield_value(value)\n```\n\nThe base implementation converts a `timedelta` to integer microseconds in [`django/db/backends/base/operations.py:590`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/backends/base/operations.py:590). That is used by non-native backends. PostgreSQL and Oracle override this method to return the `timedelta` unchanged, allowing their drivers to bind the native interval value:\n\n- [`django/db/backends/postgresql/operations.py:333`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/backends/postgresql/operations.py:333)\n- [`django/db/backends/oracle/operations.py:613`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/backends/oracle/operations.py:613)\n\nOn reads, `DurationField.get_db_converters()` checks the feature flag at [`django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:1968`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:1968). For non-native backends, it adds `connection.ops.convert_durationfield_value`, whose base implementation reconstructs a `datetime.timedelta` from the stored microsecond integer in [`django/db/backends/base/operations.py:683`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-c0-bare-django-django/django/db/backends/base/operations.py:683). Native backends omit that converter because their drivers already return duration-compatible values.\n\nIn short: `features.has_native_duration_field` chooses native versus emulated handling; `connection.data_types[\"DurationField\"]` chooses the SQL column type; and `connection.ops` handles backend-specific serialization and deserialization.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 29087, "output_tokens": 2278, "total_tokens": 31365, "cache_read_tokens": 235264, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 8, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["/base.py", "/features.py", "base.py", "django/db/backends/base/base.py", "django/db/backends/base/operations.py", "django/db/models/expressions.py", "django/db/models/fields/__init__.py"], "files_edited": [], "repowise_tools_called": [], "server_tools_called": {}, "attach_guard_fired": null, "token_source": "codex:turn.completed.usage", "category": "", "arm": "c0-bare", "arm_provenance": {"arm": "c0-bare", "server_name": null, "launch_command": null, "launch_args": null, "served_tools": null, "client_tools": [], "index": null, "shares_index_with": null, "hooks_declared": [], "isolation": "worktree", "tree": "C:\\Users\\ragha\\Desktop\\bakeoff\\lb-c0-bare-django-django"}, "served_tools": [], "served_count": null, "mcp_tools_issued": [], "mcp_isError_count": 0, "arm_exercised": null, "mcp_per_server": {}, "hook_events": null, "hook_injections": null, "index_evidence": {}, "models_used": ["gpt-5.6-sol"], "prompt_style": "neutral", "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5", "wall_clock_seconds": 94.7063159942627, "index_time_seconds": 0.0, "judge_time_seconds": 9.501171588897705, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.08854675000000001, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:44:14.743665+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Where in the codebase are the connection features that determine whether native duration field support exists, and how does DurationField resolve database-specific duration storage implementations across different backends?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 94704, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_044_c0-bare.json"}
{"task_id": "django_044", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "repowise", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "The capability flag is `connection.features.has_native_duration_field`.\n\n- [`django/db/backends/base/features.py:85`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/backends/base/features.py:85) defaults it to `False`.\n- [`django/db/backends/postgresql/features.py:17`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/backends/postgresql/features.py:17) sets it to `True`.\n- [`django/db/backends/oracle/features.py:28`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/backends/oracle/features.py:28) also sets it to `True`.\n- MySQL and SQLite inherit the `False` default.\n\n`DurationField` resolves storage through three backend abstractions:\n\n1. Database column type\n\n`DurationField.get_internal_type()` returns `\"DurationField\"` at [`django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:1943`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:1943). The connection uses that key in its backend `data_types` mapping:\n\n- PostgreSQL \u2192 `interval` in [`postgresql/base.py:115`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/backends/postgresql/base.py:115)\n- Oracle \u2192 `INTERVAL DAY(9) TO SECOND(6)` in [`oracle/base.py:135`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/backends/oracle/base.py:135)\n- MySQL \u2192 `bigint` in [`mysql/base.py:120`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/backends/mysql/base.py:120)\n- SQLite \u2192 `bigint` in [`sqlite3/base.py:75`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/backends/sqlite3/base.py:75)\n\n2. Values written to the database\n\n`DurationField.get_db_prep_value()` delegates directly to `connection.ops.adapt_durationfield_value()` at [`fields/__init__.py:1965`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:1965).\n\nThe base implementation converts a `timedelta` to integer microseconds at [`base/operations.py:590`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/backends/base/operations.py:590). MySQL and SQLite use this fallback. PostgreSQL and Oracle override the method and return the `timedelta` unchanged, allowing their drivers to bind native interval values:\n\n- [`postgresql/operations.py:333`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/backends/postgresql/operations.py:333)\n- [`oracle/operations.py:613`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/backends/oracle/operations.py:613)\n\n3. Values read from the database\n\nAt [`fields/__init__.py:1968`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:1968), `get_db_converters()` checks the feature flag. For non-native backends it adds `connection.ops.convert_durationfield_value`, whose base implementation turns the stored microsecond integer back into `datetime.timedelta` at [`base/operations.py:683`](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-repowise-full-django-django/django/db/backends/base/operations.py:683). Native backends omit this converter because their database drivers already return duration-compatible values.\n\nThus, the feature class declares native support, `data_types` selects the physical SQL type, and the operations class supplies backend-specific write/read conversion.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 25405, "output_tokens": 1820, "total_tokens": 27225, "cache_read_tokens": 169984, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 9, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": ["/base.py", "django/db/backends/base/features.py", "django/db/backends/base/operations.py", "dja