4019, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.07120425, "error": null, "timed_out": false, "retries": 0, "timestamp": "2026-08-05T15:45:59.080110+00:00", "prompt_sent": "Answer the following question about this code repository.\nBe specific and reference actual code files and functions.\n\nQUESTION: Where in the codebase are the connection features that determine whether native duration field support exists, and how does DurationField resolve database-specific duration storage implementations across different backends?\n\nThink step by step. Use the available tools to explore the codebase.", "model_used": "gpt-5.6-sol", "session_id": "", "stop_reason": "", "duration_api_ms": 75675, "repo_commit": "3b161e60964a", "raw_output_file": "logs\\layerb_codex_bakeoff_django\\raw_outputs\\django_044_repowise.json"}
{"task_id": "django_044", "benchmark": "swe_qa", "condition": "codegraph", "repo": "django/django", "question_type": "django", "resolved": null, "answer": "Native duration support is controlled by the backend feature class:\n\n- The default is `False` in [django/db/backends/base/features.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/backends/base/features.py:85).\n- PostgreSQL overrides it to `True` in [django/db/backends/postgresql/features.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/backends/postgresql/features.py:17).\n- Oracle overrides it to `True` in [django/db/backends/oracle/features.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/backends/oracle/features.py:28).\n- MySQL and SQLite inherit `False`, so Django treats their duration columns as emulated.\n\n`DurationField` itself is in [django/db/models/fields/__init__.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/models/fields/__init__.py:1926). Its docstring summarizes the strategy: native intervals on PostgreSQL and Oracle, and a `bigint` count of microseconds elsewhere.\n\nThe database column type is resolved through the normal `Field.db_type()` mechanism:\n\n1. `DurationField.get_internal_type()` returns `\"DurationField\"` at line 1943.\n2. `Field.db_type(connection)` looks that key up in `connection.data_types` at line 889.\n3. Each backend supplies its own mapping:\n\n   - PostgreSQL: `\"interval\"` in [postgresql/base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/backends/postgresql/base.py:115).\n   - Oracle: `\"INTERVAL DAY(9) TO SECOND(6)\"` in [oracle/base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/backends/oracle/base.py:135).\n   - MySQL: `\"bigint\"` in [mysql/base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/backends/mysql/base.py:120).\n   - SQLite: `\"bigint\"` in [sqlite3/base.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/backends/sqlite3/base.py:75).\n\nValue handling follows the same backend dispatch:\n\n- `DurationField.get_db_prep_value()` delegates to `connection.ops.adapt_durationfield_value()` at line 1965.\n- The base implementation in [base/operations.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/backends/base/operations.py:590) converts a `timedelta` to an integer number of microseconds using `duration_microseconds()`. This is used by emulated backends such as MySQL and SQLite.\n- PostgreSQL\u2019s override returns the `timedelta` unchanged in [postgresql/operations.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/backends/postgresql/operations.py:333), allowing its driver to bind a native interval.\n- Oracle likewise returns it unchanged in [oracle/operations.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/backends/oracle/operations.py:613).\n\nOn reads, `DurationField.get_db_converters()` checks `connection.features.has_native_duration_field` at line 1968. For non-native backends it adds `connection.ops.convert_durationfield_value`, whose base implementation constructs `datetime.timedelta(..., microseconds=value)` in [base/operations.py](C:/Users/ragha/Desktop/bakeoff/lb-codegraph-django-django/django/db/backends/base/operations.py:683). Native PostgreSQL and Oracle values need no such converter because their drivers return duration-compatible values directly.\n\nSo the feature flag controls conversion behavior, while `connection.data_types` and `connection.ops` independently provide the backend-specific SQL storage type and Python/database value adaptation.", "judge_scores": {"correctness": 9, "completeness": 9, "relevance": 10, "clarity": 9, "reasoning": 9}, "input_tokens": 40735, "output_tokens": 1804, "total_tokens": 42539, "cache_read_tokens": 147456, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "num_tool_calls": 6, "num_turns": 1, "task_subagent_calls": 0, "files_explored": 